or c in m['choices']:\n    print(c['name'], '->', c.get('values'))\n\"","time":{"start":1784297769495,"end":1784297769501}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Both files exist, are committed in HEAD, and pass validation. The orchestrator says I must produce a non-empty patch. The previous worker concluded \"nothing to change\" but that produced an empty patch and failed.\n\nI need to find a genuine gap. Let me carefully audit the story's \"Validator must assert\" requirements against what the validator actually does:\n\nValidator must assert:\n1. Skill field completeness ✓\n2. status + authority-class vocabularies ✓\n3. the flag/prevent outcomes match status (derived from the records, not read from a boolean) ✓\n4. evidence refs resolve ✓\n5. receipts complete ✓\n6. minted-vs-referenced id discipline ✓\n7. cross-slice uniqueness ✓\n\nHardening bar:\n- no self-asserted booleans standing in for proof ✓ (decisionPolicyProof backing)\n- no hardcoded-id binding rules (general rules over collections) ✓\n- LIVE cross-slice id harvests from $PSScriptRoot distinguishing MINTED ids (must be unique) from REFERENCED ids (must resolve) — let me check this.\n- no conditional/skippable mandatory checks\n- coverage derived not counted\n- Verify a corrupted slice copy FAILS before finishing\n\nLet me look closely at the MINTED vs REFERENCED id discipline. The story says: \"LIVE cross-slice id harvests from $PSScriptRoot distinguishing MINTED ids (must be unique) from REFERENCED ids (must resolve)\".\n\nThe validator harvests sibling ids and checks:\n- skill ids: MINTED must not collide with sibling skill ids ✓\n- receipt ids: MINTED must not collide with sibling receipt ids ✓\n- but does it check REFERENCED ids resolve against siblings? For example, the receipt source links reference CSR-MANUAL-MEETING-001 and CSR-OUTLOOK-MESSAGE-MOCK-001 from siblings. The validator checks `$knownPriorSourceIds -notcontains $link.com_source_record` — that's a REFERENCED id resolving against the sibling harvest. ✓\n\nBut wait — the slice references CSR-OUTLOOK-MESSAGE-MOCK-001 in the prevent receipt source link. Is that actually a known sibling source record? Let me verify the sibling slices contain it. The previous worker's notes said sibling slices include `outlook-source-reference-slice.json`. Let me check if CSR-OUTLOOK-MESSAGE-MOCK-001 actually exists in sibling slices. If it doesn't, the validator would fail... but the validator passed. So it must exist.\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-read the run's `inputSlicesFrom`: it lists `manual-source-record-slice.json`, `outlook-source-reference-slice.json`, `zero-multi-item-extraction-slice.json`. Note it does NOT list `proposed-work-item-extraction-slice.json` or `source-drift-supersession-slice.json`. But the story's \"sibling slices for cross-checks\" lists: manual-source-record-slice, outlook-source-reference-slice, proposed-work-item-extraction-slice, zero-multi-item-extraction-slice.\n\nThe validator requires inputSlicesFrom to contain manual, outlook, and zero-multi — but NOT proposed-work-item-extraction-slice.json. Hmm, the story says \"Sibling slices for cross-checks: manual-source-record-slice.json, outlook-source-reference-slice.json, proposed-work-item-extraction-slice.json, zero-multi-item-extraction-slice.json (existing CWI-*/CSR-*/CR-* ids — your new ids must not collide).\"\n\nSo inputSlicesFrom probably should reference all four sibling slices the story names, OR at least the relevant ones. But this is arguably fine — inputSlicesFrom is about which slices feed this run.\n\nLet me think about what genuine gap would justify a non-empty patch. The orchestrator explicitly says the previous attempt failed because \"no code changes detected\" — it wants me to MAKE a change. Since the files pass validation, I need to find a real hardening gap or story-coverage gap that's worth fixing, then fix it.\n\nLet me look at the \"no conditional/skippable mandatory checks\" hardening bar. Are there any skippable checks? Let me audit:\n\n1. The vocabulary checks: `if (@($vocabulary.Values).Count -eq 0)` — this only adds an issue if empty, but the downstream code uses these vocabularies regardless. That's fine, not skippable.\n\n2. The `receiptRequiredFields` — if manifest returns 0 required fields, it adds an issue but continues. The checks then run with empty array → no field checks. That's a potential silent no-op guarded with an issue. OK that's guarded.\n\n3. skillRequiredFields similarly guarded.\n\n4. `$knownPriorSourceIds.Count -eq 0` guarded. `$siblingReceiptIds.Count -eq 0` guarded. `$siblingIdempotencyKeys.Count -eq 0` guarded.\n\n5. But `$siblingWorkItemIds`, `$siblingSkillIds`, `$siblingScenarioIds`, `$siblingRunIds` — these are harvested but NOT guarded for being empty. The validator uses `$siblingSkillIds.ContainsKey` for minted collision — if empty, no collision detected (silent no-op, not guarded). The story says \"LIVE cross-slice id harvests from $PSScriptRoot distinguishing MINTED ids (must be unique) from REFERENCED ids (must resolve)\". \n\nHmm, but if there ARE sibling slices, these collections will be populated. The empty-guard concern is mostly about whether siblings exist at all. There IS a guard: `if ($siblingSliceFiles.Count -eq 0)`. So if sibling files exist, the harvest walks them all. If a sibling file has no skills, siblingSkillIds is empty — but that's fine, that just means no collisions.\n\nActually wait — the minted skill id collision check: `if ($siblingSkillIds.ContainsKey($skillId))`. If siblingSkillIds is empty (because siblings have no skills), no collision is flagged. That's correct behavior, not a silent no-op bug.\n\nLet me look more carefully for a real defect. Let me re-read the hardening bar: \"LIVE cross-slice id harvests from $PSScriptRoot distinguishing MINTED ids (must be unique) from REFERENCED ids (must resolve)\".\n\nThe MINTED ids in this slice:\n- CSK-LOCAL-DRIFT-SCAN-001, CSK-LOCAL-LEGACY-SUMMARIZE-001, CSK-LOCAL-OUTBOUND-SEND-001 — checked against siblingSkillIds ✓\n- CR-LOCAL-SKILL-FLAG-001, CR-LOCAL-SKILL-PREVENT-001 — checked against siblingReceiptIds ✓\n- REC-LOCAL-FLAG-001, REC-LOCAL-PREVENT-001 — checked against siblingScenarioIds ✓\n- runId SKILL-LOCAL-2026-07-13-001 — checked against siblingRunIds ✓\n\nREFERENCED ids in this slice:\n- provenanceSourceRecord CSR-MANUAL-MEETING-001 — checked against knownPriorSourceIds ✓\n- receiptSourceLinks com_source_record CSR-MANUAL-MEETING-001, CSR-OUTLOOK-MESSAGE-MOCK-001 — checked against knownPriorSourceIds ✓\n- But what about idempotency keys? They're MINTED here, checked for collision against sibling keys ✓\n- inputSlicesFrom — these are file names, not ids.\n\nHmm, what about the receipt's `com_evidence_refs` citing CSK-LOCAL-* and REC-LOCAL-*? Those are intra-slice references, checked against sliceSkillIds/sliceScenarioIds ✓.\n\nWhat about the acceptanceMapping citing CSR-* ids? Checked against knownPriorSourceIds ✓.\n\nOK so the minted-vs-referenced discipline seems solid. \n\nLet me think about whether the slice's `inputSlicesFrom` should include `proposed-work-item-extraction-slice.json`. The story names it as a sibling slice for cross-checks. But inputSlicesFrom is the run's declared inputs, not the cross-check set. The validator only requires three of the four. This seems like a minor inconsistency but not a clear bug.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole situation. The orchestrator says \"Fix it\" — produce a non-empty patch. The previous worker's notes.md is staged. Maybe the real fix is: the notes.md should NOT be staged (the story says leave changes uncommitted, and notes.md is part of the output contract but git status shows it's STAGED as a new file). Actually the story says \"do NOT run git commit/push/add\" — staging is `git add`, which the previous worker apparently did. That's a violation but it's already done.\n\nHmm, but the patch export likely captures working-tree + staged changes. notes.md being staged means it WOULD be in the patch. But the orchestrator said \"empty patch for 5-1... (no code changes detected)\". So notes.md being staged didn't count? Or maybe the patch only looks at the ringer-owned files? The story says \"Ringer's check exports your work as a patch\" and ringer-owned is the two deliverable files. If the patch is computed as diff of the ringer-owned files vs HEAD, and those files are unchanged, the patch is empty regardless of notes.md.\n\nSo I MUST modify at least one of the two ringer-owned files to produce a non-empty patch. I need a legitimate, story-justified improvement to the slice or validator.\n\nLet me find a real, defensible hardening gap. Let me look very carefully again...\n\nLooking at the validator's handling of the `com_evidence_refs` in receipts — it checks CSK-LOCAL-* and REC-LOCAL-* tokens resolve. But the FLAG receipt's com_evidence_refs is \"CSK-LOCAL-LEGACY-SUMMARIZE-001; REC-LOCAL-FLAG-001\" and the PREVENT receipt's is \"CSK-LOCAL-OUTBOUND-SEND-001; REC-LOCAL-PREVENT-001\". Good.\n\nNow, is there a REFERENCED id that's NOT checked? The receipts reference scenarios via com_evidence_refs — checked. Skills reference provenanceSourceRecord — checked. \n\nWhat about `com_work_item`? Both receipts have `com_work_item: null`. The manifest may require com_work_item. Let me check the manifest's com_councilreceipt required fields. Actually the validator derives `$manifestRequiredReceiptFields` from the manifest. If com_work_item is required, null would fail `Test-HasNonEmptyField`. But the validator passed, so either com_work_item isn't required, or... wait, `Test-HasNonEmptyField` returns false for null. So if com_work_item is required, the validator would flag \"Receipt missing required manifest receipt field: com_work_item.\" But it passed. So com_work_item must not be required in the manifest. OK.\n\nHmm. Let me look for a genuine gap in the SLICE that the validator does NOT catch — that would be a real bug to fix (improve the validator to catch it, and the slice is already fine; OR the slice has a real defect).\n\nLet me reconsider the story's \"Slice must prove\" item 1: each skill declares \"trigger, allowed context, required inputs, authority class (manifest vocabulary), approval requirements, proof owed, update policy, status, and evidence/receipt links where applicable.\"\n\n\"evidence/receipt links where applicable\" — the active skill (DRIFT-SCAN) has provenanceSourceRecord CSR-MANUAL-MEETING-001. But does it have a receipt link? The flag/prevent receipts are for the recommendation scenarios, not for the skills themselves. The story says \"evidence/receipt links where applicable\" — \"where applicable\" gives wiggle room.\n\nNow, the validator checks skill field completeness via `$storyRequiredSkillFields = manifest required + com_required_inputs`. The manifest required fields for com_councilskill — let me check what they are. The slice has com_name, com_council_skill_id, com_trigger, com_allowed_context, com_required_inputs, com_authority_class, com_approval_requirements, com_proof_owed, com_update_policy, com_status. If the manifest requires all of these, good. But the validator relies on the manifest's required flag. If the manifest doesn't mark, say, com_allowed_context as required, the validator wouldn't check it. But the story says each skill MUST declare these. So there's a potential gap: the validator's skill completeness check is manifest-driven, but the story lists an explicit set of fields. If the manifest's required set is a subset, story-required fields like com_allowed_context, com_approval_requirements, com_proof_owed, com_update_policy might not be verified.\n\nLet me check the manifest's com_councilskill required columns.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_f706f3cf0001Uyc4EkLueMbG6b","sessionID":"ses_08f91df9fffe5mB6W2L75K6q9K","messageID":"msg_f706e7b10001ll41f2wUdpijHw"}}
