{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1784297901940,"sessionID":"ses_08f91df9fffe5mB6W2L75K6q9K","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_d409e4484c43433887913498","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd _bmad-output/implementation-artifacts && python3 -c \"\nimport json,glob,re\nfor f in sorted(glob.glob('*.json')):\n    if f in ('dataverse-mvp-schema-manifest.json','skill-registry-slice.json'): continue\n    try: d=open(f,encoding='utf-8-sig').read()\n    except: continue\n    rec=sorted(set(re.findall(r'REC-LOCAL-[A-Z0-9-]+',d)))\n    if rec: print(f, rec)\nprint('--- done (files with REC-LOCAL-*) ---')\n\"","workdir":"/home/doug/.ringer/work/bmad-hybrid/5-1-maintain-the-minion-skill-registry"},"output":"--- done (files with REC-LOCAL-*) ---\n","metadata":{"output":"--- done (files with REC-LOCAL-*) ---\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd _bmad-output/implementation-artifacts && python3 -c \"\nimport json,glob,re\nfor f in sorted(glob.glob('*.json')):\n    if f in ('dataverse-mvp-schema-manifest.json','skill-registry-slice.json'): continue\n    try: d=open(f,encoding='utf-8-sig').read()\n    except: continue\n    rec=sorted(set(re.findall(r'REC-LOCAL-[A-Z0-9-]+',d)))\n    if rec: print(f, rec)\nprint('--- done (files with REC-LOCAL-*) ---')\n\"","time":{"start":1784297901923,"end":1784297901927}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Interesting! My new guard for siblingScenarioIds actually FLAGGED — meaning no sibling slice has any `scenarioId` property. So my guard exposed a real gap: the scenario-id collision check (line 490) was silently no-op'ing because no sibling harvests scenario ids. This is exactly the kind of latent bug the guard is meant to surface.\n\nBut wait — this means the EXISTING validator's `$siblingScenarioIds.ContainsKey($scenarioId)` check at line 490 has ALWAYS been a silent no-op (no sibling has scenarioIds). So the scenario-id collision check was never actually protecting anything. My guard correctly surfaces this.\n\nNow I have two options:\n1. Keep the guard and accept that it now fails — but that means the slice FAILS, which breaks the story. I can't ship a failing validator.\n2. The guard revealed that the scenario-id collision check is structurally non-functional because siblings don't use `scenarioId` as a property name. \n\nHmm. Let me check: do sibling slices use `scenarioId` at all? This slice uses `scenarioId`. If no other slice uses that property name, then cross-slice scenario-id collision is vacuous. The scenario ids are REC-LOCAL-* — let me check if siblings emit REC-LOCAL-* under some OTHER property.\n\nActually, the harvest collects by property NAME (\"scenarioId\"). If siblings store REC-LOCAL-* ids under a different property (e.g., inside recommendationScenarios with a different field), the harvest misses them. Let me check how REC-LOCAL-* appears across siblings.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_f7071448e001516eCR2tapFy7V","sessionID":"ses_08f91df9fffe5mB6W2L75K6q9K","messageID":"msg_f70712371001dcaRtpw24ocfJ9"}}
