 worker is staged. I need to write a fresh notes.md reflecting MY work. The story says don't run git add/commit. The previous worker staged notes.md — I should unstage it? The story says \"do NOT run git commit/push/add\". Unstaging via `git reset` touches the index but isn't add/commit/push. But the story also says \"do NOT touch .git\". `git reset` touches .git/index. Hmm. The story said \"never run git commit/push/add\" and \"do NOT touch .git\". Resetting the index technically modifies .git/index. \n\nActually the cleanest: just overwrite notes.md with my content. Whether it's staged or not, the patch export will capture it if it diffs from HEAD. notes.md is a NEW file (not in HEAD), so whether staged or unstaged, it shows as new. The previous worker's staged notes.md is stale content from the failed attempt. I'll overwrite it with my accurate notes. That's a Write tool operation, not a git command.\n\nLet me write the notes.md. It should list: what I changed, what I read for conventions, which command verifies it, assumptions/follow-ups.\n\nLet me check final git status / diff stat.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_f7072dbab001R4ydEiXMEajaQ3","sessionID":"ses_08f91df9fffe5mB6W2L75K6q9K","messageID":"msg_f7072b6b2001EPsT6szu0SFE6N"}}
