luations'][0]['candidateRows'][0]['com_decision']='bogus'\" \"T4 decision-vocab\"\necho \"--- T5: gapCitation cites row id but text from a DIFFERENT row (should FAIL substantive overlap) ---\"\nrun \"p=d['platformEvaluationRun']['customServiceProposals'][0]; p['gapCitation']='Cites the recorded contract gap in evaluation row '+p['addressesGapInRow']+' about Power Automate consumption fit and idempotency semantics unrelated to the cited row.'; \" \"T5 gap-mismatch\"\necho \"--- T6: duplicate minted PE-LOCAL id within slice (should FAIL within-slice uniq) ---\"\nrun \"d['platformEvaluationRun']['evaluations'][1]['capabilityEvaluationId']='PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001'\" \"T6 dup-pe-id\"\necho \"--- T7: candidate outside named universe (should FAIL) ---\"\nrun \"d['platformEvaluationRun']['evaluations'][0]['candidateRows'][0]['com_microsoft_candidate']='Azure OpenAI'\" \"T7 candidate-universe\""},"output":"--- baseline (should pass) ---\nPLATFORM_EVALUATION_SLICE_VALIDATE_OK\nbaseline RC=0\n--- T1: proposal decision receipt timestamp BEFORE evals (should FAIL ordering) ---\nRC=1 :: T1 ordering\nPlatform evaluation slice validation failed:\n- Custom proposal CSP-LOCAL-001 decision receipt CR-LOCAL-PE-001 was recorded at 2026-07-14T11:00:00.0000000+00:00 before Microsoft-native evaluation review receipt at 2026-07-14T13:00:00.0000000+00:00; Microsoft-native planes must be evaluated before custom substrate.\n- Custom proposal CSP-LOCAL-001 decision receipt CR-LOCAL-PE-001 was recorded at 2026-07-14T11:00:00.0000000+00:00 before Microsoft-native evaluation review receipt at 2026-07-14T13:30:00.0000000+00:00; Microsoft-native planes must be evaluated before custom substrate.\n--- T2: remove ALL eval-review evidence_refs (should FAIL no-op tripwire) ---\nRC=1 :: T2 no-op-tripwire\nPlatform evaluation slice validation failed:\n- Microsoft-native evaluated before custom substrate is unproven: no evaluation-review receipt (a receipt whose com_evidence_refs references a minted capabilityEvaluationId) was recorded before the custom proposal decision.\n--- T3: add deployedService marker (should FAIL impl-marker) ---\nRC=1 :: T3 impl-marker\nPlatform evaluation slice validation failed:\n- Slice must not contain an implementation-marker field 'deployedService'; decisions must be recorded before implementation and no implementation artifacts are referenced.\n--- T4: com_decision bogus (should FAIL vocab) ---\nRC=1 :: T4 decision-vocab\nPlatform evaluation slice validation failed:\n- Evaluation PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001 row PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001-ROW-01 com_decision must use manifest com_platformdecision vocabulary, found: bogus.\n--- T5: gapCitation cites row id but text from a DIFFERENT row (should FAIL substantive overlap) ---\nRC=0 :: T5 gap-mismatch\n--- T6: duplicate minted PE-LOCAL id within slice (should FAIL within-slice uniq) ---\nRC=1 :: T6 dup-pe-id\nPlatform evaluation slice validation failed:\n- Duplicate evaluation capabilityEvaluationId: PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001.\n- Duplicate minted PE-LOCAL-* id in slice: PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001.\n--- T7: candidate outside named universe (should FAIL) ---\nRC=1 :: T7 candidate-universe\nPlatform evaluation slice validation failed:\n- Evaluation PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001 row PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001-ROW-01 com_microsoft_candidate must come from the named candidate universe, found: Azure OpenAI.\n","metadata":{"output":"--- baseline (should pass) ---\nPLATFORM_EVALUATION_SLICE_VALIDATE_OK\nbaseline RC=0\n--- T1: proposal decision receipt timestamp BEFORE evals (should FAIL ordering) ---\nRC=1 :: T1 ordering\nPlatform evaluation slice validation failed:\n- Custom proposal CSP-LOCAL-001 decision receipt CR-LOCAL-PE-001 was recorded at 2026-07-14T11:00:00.0000000+00:00 before Microsoft-native evaluation review receipt at 2026-07-14T13:00:00.0000000+00:00; Microsoft-native planes must be evaluated before custom substrate.\n- Custom proposal CSP-LOCAL-001 decision receipt CR-LOCAL-PE-001 was recorded at 2026-07-14T11:00:00.0000000+00:00 before Microsoft-native evaluation review receipt at 2026-07-14T13:30:00.0000000+00:00; Microsoft-native planes must be evaluated before custom substrate.\n--- T2: remove ALL eval-review evidence_refs (should FAIL no-op tripwire) ---\nRC=1 :: T2 no-op-tripwire\nPlatform evaluation slice validation failed:\n- Microsoft-native evaluated before custom substrate is unproven: no evaluation-review receipt (a receipt whose com_evidence_refs references a minted capabilityEvaluationId) was recorded before the custom proposal decision.\n--- T3: add deployedService marker (should FAIL impl-marker) ---\nRC=1 :: T3 impl-marker\nPlatform evaluation slice validation failed:\n- Slice must not contain an implementation-marker field 'deployedService'; decisions must be recorded before implementation and no implementation artifacts are referenced.\n--- T4: com_decision bogus (should FAIL vocab) ---\nRC=1 :: T4 decision-vocab\nPlatform evaluation slice validation failed:\n- Evaluation PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001 row PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001-ROW-01 com_decision must use manifest com_platformdecision vocabulary, found: bogus.\n--- T5: gapCitation cites row id but text from a DIFFERENT row (should FAIL substantive overlap) ---\nRC=0 :: T5 gap-mismatch\n--- T6: duplicate minted PE-LOCAL id within slice (should FAIL within-slice uniq) ---\nRC=1 :: T6 dup-pe-id\nPlatform evaluation slice validation failed:\n- Duplicate evaluation capabilityEvaluationId: PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001.\n- Duplicate minted PE-LOCAL-* id in slice: PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001.\n--- T7: candidate outside named universe (should FAIL) ---\nRC=1 :: T7 candidate-universe\nPlatform evaluation slice validation failed:\n- Evaluation PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001 row PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001-ROW-01 com_microsoft_candidate must come from the named candidate universe, found: Azure OpenAI.\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd /home/doug/.ringer/work/bmad-hybrid/5-3-record-microsoft-platform-evaluation-evidence\ncp _bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/platform-evaluation-slice.json /tmp/pe-backup.json\nV=_bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/platform-evaluation-slice-validate.ps1\nexport DOTNET_ROOT=$HOME/.dotnet; export PATH=$HOME/.dotnet:$HOME/.dotnet/tools:$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH\nrun () { python3 -c \"\nimport json,sys\nd=json.load(open('_bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/platform-evaluation-slice.json'))\n$1\njson.dump(d,open('_bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/platform-evaluation-slice.json','w'),indent=2)\n\"\n  out=$(pwsh -File \"$V\" 2>&1); rc=$?\n  echo \"RC=$rc :: $2\"\n  if [ $rc -ne 0 ]; then echo \"$out\" | head -3; fi\n  cp /tmp/pe-backup.json _bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/platform-evaluation-slice.json\n}\necho \"--- baseline (should pass) ---\"\npwsh -File \"$V\" 2>&1 | tail -1; echo \"baseline RC=$?\"\necho \"--- T1: proposal decision receipt timestamp BEFORE evals (should FAIL ordering) ---\"\nrun \"r=d['platformEvaluationRun']['receipts']; [x.update({'com_occurred_at':'2026-07-14T07:00:00-04:00'}) for x in r if x['com_receipt_id']=='CR-LOCAL-PE-001']\" \"T1 ordering\"\necho \"--- T2: remove ALL eval-review evidence_refs (should FAIL no-op tripwire) ---\"\nrun \"r=d['platformEvaluationRun']['receipts']; [x.update({'com_evidence_refs':'customServiceProposals[CSP-LOCAL-001]'}) for x in r if x['com_receipt_id'] in ('CR-LOCAL-PE-002','CR-LOCAL-PE-003')]\" \"T2 no-op-tripwire\"\necho \"--- T3: add deployedService marker (should FAIL impl-marker) ---\"\nrun \"d['platformEvaluationRun']['deployedService']='ledger-projection-v1'\" \"T3 impl-marker\"\necho \"--- T4: com_decision bogus (should FAIL vocab) ---\"\nrun \"d['platformEvaluationRun']['evaluations'][0]['candidateRows'][0]['com_decision']='bogus'\" \"T4 decision-vocab\"\necho \"--- T5: gapCitation cites row id but text from a DIFFERENT row (should FAIL substantive overlap) ---\"\nrun \"p=d['platformEvaluationRun']['customServiceProposals'][0]; p['gapCitation']='Cites the recorded contract gap in evaluation row '+p['addressesGapInRow']+' about Power Automate consumption fit and idempotency semantics unrelated to the cited row.'; \" \"T5 gap-mismatch\"\necho \"--- T6: duplicate minted PE-LOCAL id within slice (should FAIL within-slice uniq) ---\"\nrun \"d['platformEvaluationRun']['evaluations'][1]['capabilityEvaluationId']='PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001'\" \"T6 dup-pe-id\"\necho \"--- T7: candidate outside named universe (should FAIL) ---\"\nrun \"d['platformEvaluationRun']['evaluations'][0]['candidateRows'][0]['com_microsoft_candidate']='Azure OpenAI'\" \"T7 candidate-universe\"","time":{"start":1784298053462,"end":1784298053468}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The validator is comprehensive and passes. Now I need to find a LEGITIMATE defect. Let me think very carefully about what the review swarm's kill list demands and whether the current slice/validator actually has a gap.\n\nLet me re-examine the story spec's hardening bar carefully:\n\n> Hardening bar (the review swarm's kill list): no self-asserted booleans standing in for proof, no hardcoded-id binding rules (general rules over collections), LIVE cross-slice id harvests from $PSScriptRoot distinguishing MINTED ids (must be unique) from REFERENCED ids (must resolve), no conditional/skippable mandatory checks, coverage derived not counted. Verify a corrupted slice copy FAILS before finishing.\n\nLet me scrutinize the slice and validator for actual defects:\n\n**1. The \"microsoftNativeEvaluatedBeforeCustomSubstrate\" guard as self-asserted boolean:**\nThe slice has a guard `microsoftNativeEvaluatedBeforeCustomSubstrate: true`. This is a \"self-asserted boolean.\" But the validator DOES derive the ordering from receipt timestamps (lines 590-638). So the proof is derived, not just the boolean. Good. The guard is a declaration, and the validator derives the actual ordering. That's fine.\n\n**2. The custom proposal gap citation:**\nThe proposal CSP-LOCAL-001 cites gap in row PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001-ROW-02. The validator checks: the row exists, decision is `custom_gap_build`, the gap text is non-empty, and the citation references the row id + substantively overlaps the gap text. Good.\n\n**3. Decision recorded before implementation:**\nThe proposal's decisionReceipt is CR-LOCAL-PE-001. The validator checks the receipt is minted in the slice (line 585). And the implementation-marker check (lines 640-658) walks all property names. Good.\n\nBut wait — let me look more carefully. The story says \"decision recorded (receipt) BEFORE implementation (no implementation artifacts referenced).\" The validator checks `implementationArtifactsReferenced` is empty and no implementation-marker fields. But does it verify the receipt actually PRECEDES... well, there's no implementation, so \"before implementation\" is trivially satisfied by \"no implementation.\" That's acceptable.\n\n**4. Cross-slice id harvest:**\nThe validator does LIVE `Get-ChildItem -Filter \"*-slice.json\"` in `$PSScriptRoot` excluding this slice (lines 681-685). It has a missing-sibling-files tripwire (line 686-688). It distinguishes MINTED (CR receipts minted here, checked for collision with prior — lines 714-718) from REFERENCED (CR/CWI/CSR ids in slice text not minted here must resolve — lines 720-737). Good.\n\nWait, there's a subtle issue. Let me look at the MINTED check more carefully.\n\n```powershell\n$mintedCrIds = [System.Collections.Generic.HashSet[string]]::new()\nforeach ($receiptId in @($sliceReceiptIds.Keys)) { [void]$mintedCrIds.Add($receiptId) }\n```\n\nSo `$mintedCrIds` = the CR receipt ids minted in THIS slice (CR-LOCAL-PE-001/002/003).\n\n```powershell\nforeach ($mintedCr in $mintedCrIds) {\n  if ($priorCrIds -contains $mintedCr) {\n    Add-Issue $issues \"Minted receipt id $mintedCr collides with an id already present in a sibling slice or demo evidence; new CR-LOCAL-* ids must be unique across ALL slices.\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis checks that minted CR ids don't collide with sibling/demo ids. Good.\n\n```powershell\nforeach ($cr in @($sliceTextCr | Sort-Object -Unique)) {\n  if (-not $mintedCrIds.Contains($cr)) {\n    if ($priorCrIds -notcontains $cr) {\n      Add-Issue $issues \"Referenced receipt id $cr does not resolve to any sibling slice or demo evidence; referenced ids must resolve.\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis checks that any CR id in the slice text that is NOT minted here must resolve to a sibling. Good.\n\nSo the cross-slice logic seems sound.\n\nHmm. Let me think about what could actually be a real defect. Let me reconsider the story requirements vs the slice more carefully.\n\n**Story requirement: \"each candidate row capturing tenant gates, permission/DLP impact, licensing/cost, ALM path, contract gaps, decision, review reference.\"**\n\nThe slice rows have: com_tenant_gate, com_permission_dlp_impact, com_licensing_cost_gate, com_lifecycle_alm_path, com_contract_gaps, com_decision, com_review_reference. ✓ All present.\n\n**Story requirement: \"One custom-service proposal citing the specific Microsoft-native gap or tenant constraint it addresses, decision recorded (receipt) BEFORE implementation (no implementation artifacts referenced).\"**\n\nThe proposal cites gap in row PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001-ROW-02 AND tenantConstraintCited. Decision receipt CR-LOCAL-PE-001. implementationArtifactsReferenced is []. ✓\n\n**Validator must assert: \"Candidate coverage (>=4 per evaluation, from the named set)\"** ✓ (lines 362-364, 396-398)\n\n**\"row field completeness\"** ✓ (lines 385-389)\n\n**\"the custom proposal's gap citation references a recorded gap in an evaluation row\"** ✓ (lines 441-487)\n\n**\"decision receipts complete and precede any implementation marker\"** — the validator checks receipt completeness and checks no implementation markers exist. ✓\n\n**\"minted-vs-referenced ids\"** ✓\n\n**\"cross-slice uniqueness\"** ✓\n\nOK so the slice and validator appear to fully satisfy the story spec. The validator passes. So what legitimate change can I make?\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider. The Ringer check framework expects a non-empty patch. The previous worker's mistake was concluding \"no changes needed.\" But maybe there IS a subtle defect that a rigorous review would catch.\n\nLet me look very carefully for actual bugs in the validator logic:\n\n**Bug candidate 1: The `evalReviewTimestamps` ordering check.**\n\nLooking at lines 596-638:\n\n```powershell\n$proposalDecisionReceiptIds = @{}\nforeach ($proposal in $proposals) {\n  $dr = [string]$proposal.decisionReceipt\n  if (-not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($dr)) { $proposalDecisionReceiptIds[$dr] = $true }\n}\n$evalReviewTimestamps = [System.Collections.Generic.List[datetimeoffset]]::new()\nforeach ($receipt in $receipts) {\n  $receiptId = [string]$receipt.com_receipt_id\n  if ($proposalDecisionReceiptIds.ContainsKey($receiptId)) { continue }\n  $evrefs = [string]$receipt.com_evidence_refs\n  if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($evrefs)) { continue }\n  $matchesEval = $false\n  foreach ($evaluation in $evaluations) {\n    $eid = [string]$evaluation.capabilityEvaluationId\n    if (-not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($eid) -and $evrefs -match [regex]::Escape($eid)) {\n      $matchesEval = $true\n      break\n    }\n  }\n  if ($matchesEval -and $receiptTimestamps.ContainsKey($receiptId)) {\n    $evalReviewTimestamps.Add($receiptTimestamps[$receiptId]) | Out-Null\n  }\n}\n```\n\nSo eval-review receipts are those whose `com_evidence_refs` references a minted `capabilityEvaluationId`, excluding the proposal decision receipts themselves. Then it asserts every custom proposal decision receipt was recorded at or after EVERY eval-review receipt.\n\nIn the slice:\n- CR-LOCAL-PE-002 (reviewed, eval 1): evidence_refs = \"evaluations[PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001]; customServiceProposals[CSP-LOCAL-001]\" — references PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001 ✓. Timestamp 09:00.\n- CR-LOCAL-PE-003 (reviewed, eval 2): evidence_refs = \"evaluations[PE-LOCAL-EVAL-002]\" — references PE-LOCAL-EVAL-002 ✓. Timestamp 09:30.\n- CR-LOCAL-PE-001 (proposed, custom proposal): decisionReceipt for CSP-LOCAL-001. Timestamp 10:00.\n\nSo eval-review timestamps = [09:00, 09:30]. Proposal timestamp = 10:00. 10:00 >= 09:00 and 10:00 >= 09:30. ✓ Passes.\n\nThis is correct and well-designed.\n\n**Bug candidate 2: The `com_official_source_date_checked` field.**\n\nThe rows have `com_official_source_date_checked: \"2026-07-07\"`. Is this a manifest-required field? The validator's `rowCompletenessFields` is the union of manifest-required PE columns + the story-mandated 6 fields. If `com_official_source_date_checked` is manifest-required, it's checked. If not, it's NOT in the story-mandated set. Let me check the manifest.\n\nActually wait — is `com_official_source_date_checked` even a real manifest field? The story says rows capture \"tenant gates, permission/DLP impact, licensing/cost, ALM path, contract gaps, decision, review reference.\" It doesn't mention `com_official_source_date_checked`. But the slice includes it. Is it a manifest field? Let me check the manifest for the com_councilplatformevaluation table.\n\nHmm, this is a potential issue but probably not — the slice is allowed to carry extra fields. The validator checks the UNION of manifest-required + story-mandated. Extra fields are fine.\n\nLet me focus on finding a REAL defect. Let me check the manifest to verify the field names and the `com_platformdecision` vocabulary (the decision values).\n\nActually, let me step back and think about this differently. The task says the previous attempt FAILED with \"empty patch.\" The fix required is to produce an actual code change. The most honest interpretation: the files, while passing the validator, may have a genuine weakness that a deeper review would flag. OR — and this is important — the files might have been \"review-hardened\" but the reviewer found something.\n\nWait, let me re-read the failure context. The previous attempt's notes said: \"the committed owned files are the intended deliverable state. They were produced and review-hardened in the prior epic-5 wave.\" But the check framework wants a patch. So maybe the intent is that I should NOT trust the committed state and should actually IMPROVE the files.\n\nLet me look for genuine weaknesses more carefully. Let me check:\n\n1. Does the validator verify the `com_official_source_date_checked` is a valid date? No — it's not in any completeness or format check unless it's manifest-required. This could be a gap: the slice asserts a \"date checked\" but the validator doesn't verify it's a real ISO date. But this is minor.\n\n2. Does the validator check that `com_actor_id` matches the run's `actorId`? No. Minor.\n\n3. **The big one — let me look at the `com_confidence` and the receipt for CR-LOCAL-PE-001.** The receipt has com_confidence 0.8. Fine.\n\nLet me think about the review swarm's kill list again very precisely:\n\n> no self-asserted booleans standing in for proof\n\nThe slice declares `microsoftNativeEvaluatedBeforeCustomSubstrate: true` AND `decisionsRecordedBeforeImplementation: true` AND `noImplementationArtifactsReferenced: true`. These are self-asserted booleans. The validator derives:\n- microsoftNativeEvaluatedBeforeCustomSubstrate → derived from receipt timestamps ✓\n- noImplementationArtifactsReferenced → derived from walking property names ✓\n- decisionsRecordedBeforeImplementation → ??? \n\nFor `decisionsRecordedBeforeImplementation`: the validator checks that there are no implementation markers and that the proposal has a decisionReceipt. But does it derive that the decision was recorded \"before implementation\"? Since there's no implementation, it's vacuously true. That's acceptable — there's nothing to be \"before.\"\n\nHmm, what about `customServicesMustCiteMicrosoftGap: true`? This is derived — the validator checks the proposal cites a recorded gap. ✓\n\nOK, let me look for an actual concrete defect rather than theoretical. Let me check the manifest vocabulary for `com_platformdecision` to ensure the decisions used (defer, custom_gap_build, adopt, reject) are all valid manifest values. And check `com_receiptverb` for \"proposed\" and \"reviewed\". And `com_actortype` for \"human\". And `com_receiptresult` for \"accepted\".\n\nLet me read the manifest. Let me also check the tenant-decision-packet.json since the slice references decisions from it (fabricIqGraphPhase2, powerAppsMcpAgentFeedEvaluation, humanApprovalOwner).\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole approach. The check framework wants a code change. Let me look for a DEFENSIBLE improvement that strengthens the slice/validator against the review kill list. \n\nOne thing I notice: **The validator does NOT verify that the `com_official_source_date_checked` values are valid ISO 8601 dates.** The slice has these as \"2026-07-07\" (date only, no time). This is a candidate-row field that represents when the Microsoft candidate's official source was checked. A review might flag that these dates are unchecked strings. But this is weak.\n\nLet me look for something more concrete. Let me re-read the slice's receipt timestamps and the ordering:\n\nCR-LOCAL-PE-001 (proposed): 2026-07-14T10:00:00-04:00\nCR-LOCAL-PE-002 (reviewed eval 1): 2026-07-14T09:00:00-04:00\nCR-LOCAL-PE-003 (reviewed eval 2): 2026-07-14T09:30:00-04:00\n\nThe receipts are listed in the slice in order: 001, 002, 003. But chronologically: 002 (09:00), 003 (09:30), 001 (10:00). The list order doesn't match chronological order. That's fine for append-only receipts (they can be listed in any order; ordering is derived from timestamps).\n\nHmm. Let me think about whether there's an issue with the `com_idempotency_key` uniqueness across slices. The validator checks within-slice idempotency key uniqueness (lines 553-559) but NOT cross-slice. Is that a gap? The story says \"minted-vs-referenced ids; cross-slice uniqueness\" — that's about IDs, not idempotency keys. Idempotency keys are a receipt-level concept. The gold standard (source-drift) also only checks within-slice idempotency key uniqueness. So this matches the pattern. Not a defect.\n\nLet me look at the manifest now to verify vocabularies and check for a real mismatch. Let me also check tenant-decision-packet.json.\n\nActually — let me reconsider. Let me look very carefully at one thing: the story says the custom proposal must cite \"the specific Microsoft-native gap OR tenant constraint it addresses.\" The validator checks BOTH gapCitation (references recorded gap) AND tenantConstraintCited (non-empty). Good. But does the tenantConstraintCited actually REFERENCE a real tenant decision? The slice says \"Respects the tenant decision in tenant-decision-packet.json to defer Fabric IQ/Graph to phase 2 (fabricIqGraphPhase2=defer_to_phase_2)\". The validator only checks tenantConstraintCited is non-empty (line 489-491). It does NOT verify the cited tenant decision actually exists in tenant-decision-packet.json. \n\nThis is a potential weakness but the story spec's validator requirements list: \"the custom proposal's gap citation references a recorded gap in an evaluation row\" — it specifically says GAP citation. It doesn't require validating the tenant constraint citation resolves. So this matches the spec. Not a defect per the story spec.\n\nHmm, let me re-read the story spec's \"Validator must assert\" section:\n\n> ## Validator must assert\n> Candidate coverage (>=4 per evaluation, from the named set); row field completeness; the custom proposal's gap citation references a recorded gap in an evaluation row; decision receipts complete and precede any implementation marker; minted-vs-referenced ids; cross-slice uniqueness.\n\nSo the required assertions are:\n1. Candidate coverage (>=4 per evaluation, from named set) ✓\n2. Row field completeness ✓\n3. Custom proposal's gap citation references a recorded gap in an evaluation row ✓\n4. Decision receipts complete and precede any implementation marker ✓\n5. Minted-vs-referenced ids ✓\n6. Cross-slice uniqueness ✓\n\nAll covered. \n\nOK, so the validator fully satisfies the story spec. The slice fully satisfies the story spec. Both pass.\n\nGiven this, what legitimate change do I make to produce a non-empty patch?\n\nLet me reconsider the situation. The Ringer check wants a non-empty patch. The files are committed and pass. There must be SOME legitimate improvement, OR the check is fundamentally expecting me to re-derive/re-create the files.\n\nActually — wait. Let me re-read the task framing: \"Previous attempt failed: ... no code changes detected.\" And the instructions say \"make the code change the story requires.\" \n\nPerhaps the real issue is that the committed files, while passing, have a SUBTLE defect that the review swarm would actually kill. Let me look MUCH more carefully. The previous worker claimed hardening but may have missed something. Let me be a rigorous reviewer.\n\nLet me look at the **corrupted-copy tripwire** claim. The previous worker claimed: \"removing a candidate row from PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001 makes the validator exit 1 with issues ('must consider at least four Microsoft candidates, found 3').\" Let me verify this is actually true by testing it.\n\nLet me also test: what if I corrupt the slice in ways that SHOULD fail but might not (because the validator has a no-op hole)?\n\nSpecifically, let me test these corruption scenarios:\n1. Remove a candidate row (should fail coverage) — previous worker claims it passes\n2. Change a decision to an invalid value (should fail vocab)\n3. Remove the gapCitation (should fail)\n4. Change addressesGapInRow to a non-existent row (should fail)\n5. Make the custom proposal decision receipt timestamp EARLIER than an eval review (should fail ordering)\n6. Add an implementation-marker field (should fail)\n7. Duplicate a minted id across slices — hard to test\n\nLet me actually run these corruption tests to find a hole. If I find a corruption that SHOULD fail but DOESN'T, that's a legitimate defect to fix.\n\nActually, let me be strategic. Let me think about what the review swarm cares about and find a real hole. \n\nLet me re-examine the **minted-vs-referenced** logic very carefully:\n\n```powershell\n$mintedCrIds = ...  # CR receipt ids minted in this slice\n\nforeach ($cr in @($sliceTextCr | Sort-Object -Unique)) {\n  if (-not $mintedCrIds.Contains($cr)) {\n    if ($priorCrIds -notcontains $cr) {\n      Add-Issue \"Referenced receipt id $cr does not resolve...\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nSo a CR id in the slice text that is minted here → OK (no collision check needed beyond the minted collision check). A CR id in the slice text NOT minted here → must resolve to a sibling.\n\nNow, the MINTED collision check:\n```powershell\nforeach ($mintedCr in $mintedCrIds) {\n  if ($priorCrIds -contains $mintedCr) {\n    Add-Issue \"Minted receipt id $mintedCr collides...\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis checks minted CR ids against prior (sibling+demo) CR ids. Good.\n\nBut wait — `$priorCrIds` is collected from sibling slices via `Collect-Ids -Node $slice -Pattern \"CR-[A-Za-z0-9-]+\"`. This collects ALL CR-* strings from sibling slices, including their REFERENCED ids and their MINTED ids. So if a sibling slice references a CR id that happens to match one of my minted ids, it would flag a collision. That's fine — more conservative.\n\nBut here's a subtle thing: `$mintedCrIds` only contains the `com_receipt_id` values from THIS slice's receipts. What about the PE-LOCAL-* and CSP-LOCAL-* ids? Those are minted in this slice too. The within-slice uniqueness check (lines 743-769) handles those. But the CROSS-slice collision check for PE-LOCAL-* and CSP-LOCAL-* ids is NOT done!\n\nWait, let me re-read. The cross-slice harvest collects `priorCwiIds`, `priorCrIds`, `priorCsrIds` (CWI, CR, CSR patterns). It does NOT collect PE-LOCAL-* or CSP-LOCAL-* patterns. So:\n- Minted CR ids (CR-LOCAL-PE-*) → checked for cross-slice collision ✓\n- Minted PE-LOCAL-* ids → checked for within-slice uniqueness only, NOT cross-slice ✗\n- Minted CSP-LOCAL-* ids → checked for within-slice uniqueness only, NOT cross-slice ✗\n\nIs this a defect? The story says \"minted-vs-referenced ids; cross-slice uniqueness.\" The PE-LOCAL-* and CSP-LOCAL-* ids are minted in this slice. If a sibling slice (in a future epic) also minted a PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001, this validator wouldn't catch it. \n\nBUT — the pattern `CR-` would match `CR-LOCAL-PE-001`? No! `CR-LOCAL-PE-001` starts with `CR-` so the regex `CR-[A-Za-z0-9-]+` WOULD match it. Wait, let me check. The regex is `CR-[A-Za-z0-9-]+`. The string \"CR-LOCAL-PE-001\" — does it match? `CR-` then `LOCAL-PE-001` (all in [A-Za-z0-9-]). YES, it matches! So `Collect-Ids -Pattern \"CR-[A-Za-z0-9-]+\"` on this slice's text WOULD collect \"CR-LOCAL-PE-001\", \"CR-LOCAL-PE-002\", \"CR-LOCAL-PE-003\".\n\nWait, but those are the minted CR receipt ids. So `$sliceTextCr` includes them. And `$mintedCrIds` includes them (they're in `$sliceReceiptIds.Keys`). So for each of them: `if (-not $mintedCrIds.Contains($cr))` → false → skip. So they're treated as minted. Good.\n\nAnd the minted collision check: `foreach ($mintedCr in $mintedCrIds) { if ($priorCrIds -contains $mintedCr) ... }`. So CR-LOCAL-PE-001 is checked against prior CR ids. Good.\n\nNow what about PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001, PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001-ROW-01, CSP-LOCAL-001? These do NOT start with CWI-, CR-, or CSR-. So they're NOT harvested from sibling slices, and NOT checked for cross-slice collision. They're only checked for within-slice uniqueness (lines 743-769).\n\nIs this a real defect? The story's cross-slice uniqueness requirement... The sibling slices use CWI-/CR-/CSR- prefixes (per the hard rules: \"new CWI-LOCAL-* / CR-LOCAL-* / CSR-* ids, unique across ALL slices\"). The PE-LOCAL-* and CSP-LOCAL-* namespaces are specific to THIS story (platform evaluation). No other story would mint PE-LOCAL-* ids. So cross-slice collision for PE-LOCAL-* is theoretically impossible by namespace convention.\n\nBut the hardening bar says \"no hardcoded-id binding rules (general rules over collections).\" The current approach of only harvesting CWI/CR/CSR is a GENERAL rule over the id patterns that appear across the ecosystem. PE-LOCAL-* and CSP-LOCAL-* are story-local namespaces. This is defensible.\n\nHmm, but actually — is there a gap? Let me reconsider. The story says new ids must be \"unique across ALL slices.\" The PE-LOCAL-* ids ARE new ids minted in this slice. If I want to be rigorous, I should verify they don't collide across slices. The validator does NOT do this for PE-LOCAL-*/CSP-LOCAL-*.\n\nThis is a defensible improvement! I could add a cross-slice collision check for the PE-LOCAL-* and CSP-LOCAL-* namespaces by harvesting them from sibling slices and asserting this slice's minted ones don't collide.\n\nBut wait — would this be a \"hardcoded-id binding rule\"? No — it's a general rule: harvest all `PE-LOCAL-[A-Za-z0-9-]+` and `CSP-LOCAL-[A-Za-z0-9-]+` from sibling slices, check this slice's minted ones against them. That's a collection-driven check, not a hardcoded-id rule. This strengthens the \"cross-slice uniqueness\" assertion to cover ALL minted id namespaces, not just CWI/CR/CSR.\n\nThis is a LEGITIMATE hardening improvement that aligns with the story's \"minted-vs-referenced ids; cross-slice uniqueness\" requirement and the review kill list. It's not spurious.\n\nBut is it actually a \"defect\"? The story says ids must be unique across ALL slices. The current validator only checks CWI/CR/CSR cross-slice uniqueness. PE-LOCAL-* and CSP-LOCAL-* are only within-slice checked. A strict reviewer could argue this is a gap: \"you mint PE-LOCAL-* and CSP-LOCAL-* ids but don't verify their cross-slice uniqueness.\"\n\nHmm, but the counterargument: PE-LOCAL-* and CSP-LOCAL-* are by-convention unique to this story (no other story mints them), so the check is redundant. And the existing pattern (source-drift gold standard) only checks the CWI/CR/CSR namespaces it shares with siblings.\n\nI think this is a defensible but somewhat weak improvement. Let me look for something stronger and more clearly a defect.\n\nLet me actually look at the manifest and tenant-decision-packet to find concrete mismatches. Let me also run the corruption tests to find a no-op hole.\n\nLet me test corruption scenarios to find a real hole. If I find a corruption that SHOULD fail but the validator PASSES, that's a clear defect.\n\nLet me test:\n1. Remove the gapCitation's row id reference but keep the text — does it still pass?\n2. Set the custom proposal decision receipt to a non-existent receipt — should fail.\n3. Make the proposal decision receipt timestamp earlier than eval reviews — should fail ordering.\n4. Remove a row's com_review_reference — should fail completeness (it's in storyMandatedRowFields).\n\nActually, let me look for a smarter hole. The `evalReviewTimestamps` logic excludes proposal decision receipts. What if a proposal decision receipt's `com_evidence_refs` references an evaluation id? The logic explicitly excludes it (line 608: `if ($proposalDecisionReceiptIds.ContainsKey($receiptId)) { continue }`). Good — that prevents the proposal receipt from being counted as an eval-review receipt.\n\nBut what if there are NO eval-review receipts at all (e.g., someone removes the evidence_refs eval references)? Then `$evalReviewTimestamps.Count -eq 0` and the check at line 624 flags it. Good — no-op tripwire exists.\n\nLet me think about the **com_official_source_date_checked** field again. The slice has it on every row as \"2026-07-07\". Is it manifest-required? If yes, the validator checks it's non-empty (via completeness). If it's manifest-required, should the validator also check it's a valid date? The story doesn't explicitly require that. But it's a \"date checked\" field — an invalid date would be meaningless. Hmm, this is weak.\n\nLet me look at the manifest to understand the field structure. Let me read it.\n\nActually, let me take a different, more rigorous approach. Let me look at what the REVIEW SWARM actually killed in the previous epic-5 wave, to understand the bar. The git log says \"068ce1b epic 5: review-hardened validators for 5-1/5-2/5-3/5-5/5-6 (5-4 re-fix pending — orchestrator probe disproved its fabrication scan)\". So 5-3 WAS review-hardened. The previous worker was right that it's hardened.\n\nBut the Ringer check wants a patch. So either:\n(a) There's a remaining defect the review missed, OR\n(b) The check framework fundamentally requires a re-derivation, OR\n(c) I need to make a legitimate hardening improvement.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, the SAFEST approach that satisfies the check AND stays within story scope is to find a genuine hardening gap and fix it. Let me look harder.\n\nLet me read the manifest to check the `com_platformdecision` values and `com_councilplatformevaluation` columns. And the tenant-decision-packet. Let me also look at whether `com_official_source_date_checked` is manifest-required and whether its date format is validated.\n\nActually — I just realized something. Let me look at the `com_official_source_date_checked` field. The validator's `rowCompletenessFields` is the UNION of manifest-required PE columns + storyMandatedRowFields. If `com_official_source_date_checked` is manifest-required, it's in the completeness check. But the validator does NOT validate its DATE FORMAT. Every row carries \"2026-07-07\" — a date with no time component. Is this ISO 8601? Technically \"2026-07-07\" is a valid ISO 8601 date. But the validator's `Test-IsoTimestamp` requires the full `T...offset` format. The validator doesn't apply `Test-IsoTimestamp` to `com_official_source_date_checked`. \n\nThis is a minor gap but maybe not what the review would kill on.\n\nLet me take yet another angle. Let me look for an ACTUAL BUG in the validator — a logic error. Let me trace through the gap citation check very carefully:\n\n```powershell\n$gapRowId = [string]$proposal.addressesGapInRow\n...\nelseif ($allRowIds -notcontains $gapRowId) {\n  Add-Issue \"$pSubject addressesGapInRow must reference a real evaluation candidate row minted in this slice, found: $gapRowId.\"\n}\nelse {\n  $gapDecision = [string]$allRowIdToDecision[$gapRowId]\n  if ($gapDecision -ne \"custom_gap_build\") {\n    Add-Issue \"$pSubject addressesGapInRow must point to a row whose com_decision records a gap (custom_gap_build); row $gapRowId decision is '$gapDecision'.\"\n  }\n  $gapText = [string]$allRowIdToGaps[$gapRowId]\n  if (-not (Test-HasNonEmptyField -Record $proposal -Field \"gapCitation\")) {\n    Add-Issue \"$pSubject must carry a non-empty gapCitation.\"\n  }\n  else {\n    $citation = [string]$proposal.gapCitation\n    if ($citation -notmatch [regex]::Escape($gapRowId)) {\n      Add-Issue \"$pSubject gapCitation must cite the recorded gap row by id ($gapRowId).\"\n    }\n    ...\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis is fine. The gap citation must: reference the row by id, the row must have decision custom_gap_build, the row must have non-empty gap text, and the citation must substantively overlap the gap text.\n\nHmm OK. Let me look at whether `$allRowIdToDecision` / `$allRowIdToGaps` / `$allRowIdToCapability` are correctly populated. They're populated inside the row loop (lines 408-412) only `if (-not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($rowId))`. Fine.\n\nLet me now seriously consider the cross-slice PE-LOCAL/CSP-LOCAL uniqueness gap. Actually, let me reconsider whether it's truly a gap.\n\nThe hard rules say: \"IDs: new CWI-LOCAL-* / CR-LOCAL-* / CSR-* ids, unique across ALL slices.\"\n\nNote: this lists CWI-LOCAL-*, CR-LOCAL-*, CSR-*. It does NOT mention PE-LOCAL-* or CSP-LOCAL-*. So by the hard rules, the cross-slice uniqueness requirement applies to CWI/CR/CSR ids. The PE-LOCAL-* and CSP-LOCAL-* are story-local structural ids (evaluation ids, row ids, proposal ids) — they're not in the CWI/CR/CSR cross-slice uniqueness scope.\n\nBUT the story spec's validator requirements say \"minted-vs-referenced ids; cross-slice uniqueness\" without limiting to CWI/CR/CSR. Hmm.\n\nAnd the slice itself uses CR-LOCAL-PE-* for receipts (which ARE in the CR-* namespace and ARE cross-slice checked). The PE-LOCAL-* and CSP-LOCAL-* are structural.\n\nI think the cross-slice PE/CSP check is a defensible improvement but the current state isn't clearly defective.\n\nLet me look at this from a completely different angle. Let me actually run corruption tests to find a real hole. That's the most empirical way to find a defect.\n\nLet me test several corruptions. I'll make a copy, corrupt it, run the validator, and see if it catches it. If a corruption that SHOULD fail passes, that's a real defect.\n\nLet me do this systematically. I'll copy the slice to a temp location, corrupt it, and run the validator pointed at the corrupt copy. But the validator uses `$PSScriptRoot` for the slice path by default. I can pass `-PlatformSlicePath` to point at the corrupt copy. But the cross-slice harvest uses `$PSScriptRoot` for siblings — that's fine, siblings stay the same.\n\nWait, but actually the validator's `param` has `-PlatformSlicePath` defaulting to `$PSScriptRoot\\platform-evaluation-slice.json`. So I can pass a corrupt copy path. But the cross-slice harvest excludes `$sliceFileName = Split-Path -Leaf $PlatformSlicePath`, so if I name my corrupt copy differently (e.g., `corrupt-slice.json`), it would NOT be excluded from the sibling harvest — meaning my corrupt copy's ids would be harvested as \"prior\" ids, which could cause false collisions. Let me name it `platform-evaluation-slice.json` in a temp dir... no, the sibling harvest is in `$PSScriptRoot`.\n\nSimplest: make the corrupt copy in /tmp, copy the validator there too, or just temporarily corrupt the actual file, run, then restore. Since this is a worktree and I'll restore, that's fine. Actually, let me just back up the original, corrupt, test, restore.\n\nLet me do the corruption tests. I'll test the most likely holes.\n\nActually, let me focus. I've spent a lot of analysis. Let me empirically find a hole by testing corruptions. Let me test these:\n1. Remove the proposal's gapCitation row-id mention (keep substantive text) → should fail \"must cite by id\"\n2. Set addressesGapInRow to a row whose decision is NOT custom_gap_build → should fail\n3. Make the proposal decision receipt (CR-LOCAL-PE-001) timestamp 08:00 (before evals) → should fail ordering\n4. Remove ALL eval-review evidence_refs (so no eval-review receipts) → should fail no-op tripwire\n5. Add a `deployedService` field → should fail implementation marker\n6. Set a row's com_decision to \"bogus\" → should fail vocab\n7. **Remove the tenantConstraintCited** → should fail (non-empty check)\n\nAnd the key one I want to test:\n8. **Make the proposal's gapCitation cite the row id but reference a DIFFERENT row's gap text** (mismatch) → does it fail? The check requires substantive overlap with the CITED row's gap text. If I point addressesGapInRow at row 02 but cite row 03's gap text, the verbatim/significant-term check is against row 02's gap text. So a mismatch should fail. Let me verify.\n\nActually, let me focus on finding a genuine hole. Let me run the tests.\n\nLet me be efficient and test multiple corruptions in parallel by making copies.\n\nActually, the cleanest approach: write a test script that, for each corruption, makes a temp copy of the slice, applies the corruption via a small PowerShell/python snippet, runs the validator with -PlatformSlicePath pointing at the copy, and reports pass/fail. But the sibling harvest issue: if my copy is in /tmp, the validator's `$PSScriptRoot` is /tmp (where the validator script lives)... no, the validator is in the repo. If I run `pwsh -File <repo>/validator.ps1 -PlatformSlicePath /tmp/corrupt.json`, then `$PSScriptRoot` = the repo dir (where the validator lives), and siblings are harvested from the repo. Good. And the corrupt copy at /tmp/corrupt.json is NOT in $PSScriptRoot so it won't be harvested as a sibling. \n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety. The validator at line 680: `$sliceFileName = Split-Path -Leaf $PlatformSlicePath`. If PlatformSlicePath = /tmp/corrupt.json, sliceFileName = \"corrupt.json\". The sibling harvest excludes files named \"corrupt.json\" — but there are none, so all `*-slice.json` in repo are harvested as siblings, INCLUDING the real `platform-evaluation-slice.json`. That means the real slice's ids (CR-LOCAL-PE-*, PE-LOCAL-*) would be in `priorCrIds`. Then the corrupt copy's minted CR ids (CR-LOCAL-PE-*) would COLLIDE with the real slice's ids → false failure!\n\nSo I can't easily test with a differently-named copy in /tmp because the real slice is still in $PSScriptRoot and would be harvested as a sibling, causing collisions.\n\nAlternative: test by corrupting the ACTUAL slice file in place, running the validator, then restoring from git. Since `git checkout` would restore, and I'm not committing, this is safe. Let me do that.\n\nPlan:\n1. Back up: `cp platform-evaluation-slice.json /tmp/pe-slice-backup.json`\n2. For each corruption: apply via python, run validator, record result, restore from backup.\n3. Restore original at the end: `cp /tmp/pe-slice-backup.json platform-evaluation-slice.json` and `git checkout` to be sure.\n\nLet me write a script that does this. Actually, let me just do it inline with bash + python for each corruption. Let me be efficient and test the most likely holes.\n\nLet me write one bash command that runs multiple corruption tests.\n\nActually, I realize I should think about what hole would be most valuable to find. The review kill list emphasizes:\n- \"no self-asserted booleans standing in for proof\"\n- \"no conditional/skippable mandatory checks\"\n- \"coverage derived not counted\"\n\nA \"conditional/skippable mandatory check\" hole would be: a check that's guarded by an `if` that could silently skip. Let me look for those.\n\nLooking at the validator, many checks are guarded. E.g., the gap citation check is inside `elseif ($allRowIds -notcontains $gapRowId) { ... } else { ... }`. If `$allRowIds` is empty (no rows), then `$allRowIds -notcontains $gapRowId` is true → it would flag \"must reference a real row.\" But if rows exist but the proposal's addressesGapInRow is empty, line 442-444 catches it. OK.\n\nWhat about the eval-review ordering check no-op tripwire? Lines 623-625: `if ($proposals.Count -gt 0) { if ($evalReviewTimestamps.Count -eq 0) { Add-Issue ... } }`. Good — if there are proposals but no eval-review receipts, it flags. \n\nHmm, what if `$receiptTimestamps` doesn't contain the proposal's decision receipt (e.g., the timestamp was unparseable)? Line 630: `if (-not $receiptTimestamps.ContainsKey($dr)) { continue }`. So if the proposal's decision receipt timestamp is missing/unparseable, the ordering check is SKIPPED for that proposal. But the timestamp itself would have been flagged by `Test-IsoTimestamp` earlier (line 548). So it's not silently skipped — there's an issue for the bad timestamp. OK, defensible.\n\nLet me think about a different hole: **the `com_actor_type`/`com_actor_id` for receipts.** The validator checks `com_actor_type` is in vocab (line 542). It checks `com_actor_id` is... not explicitly. It's in `receiptRequiredFields` only if manifest-required. Let me check the manifest.\n\nOK I'm going in circles. Let me just empirically test corruptions AND read the manifest + tenant packet in parallel. Then I'll decide on the fix.\n\nLet me run the corruption tests and read the manifest.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_f707336d0001z8cUHGzHNZGVnX","sessionID":"ses_08f914172ffeudlWP9EWlAXTjJ","messageID":"msg_f707001f0001RC0ihUY5ES48cd"}}
