{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1784298214423,"sessionID":"ses_08f91718dffe8QIoOmeSnVHPPW","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-afccd94a12fbd6f2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cp _bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/phase2-projections-slice.json /tmp/opencode/phase2-slice-backup.json && echo \"backup created\"","workdir":"/home/doug/.ringer/work/bmad-hybrid/5-6-manage-phase-2-knowledge-and-analytics-projections"},"output":"backup created\n","metadata":{"output":"backup created\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cp _bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/phase2-projections-slice.json /tmp/opencode/phase2-slice-backup.json && echo \"backup created\"","time":{"start":1784298214416,"end":1784298214420}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The hardened validator passes with the hardened slice. Now let me verify the corruption test — the story requires \"Verify a corrupted slice copy FAILS before finishing.\" Let me do an in-place corruption test: flip the denial receipt's `com_decision_rationale` to remove `mvp-contracts-stable` (which should trigger my new check C), and also test a more aggressive corruption.\n\nLet me do a careful corruption test:\n1. Back up the slice.\n2. Corrupt: remove \"mvp-contracts-stable\" from the denial receipt's com_decision_rationale → should fail check (C).\n3. Restore.\n4. Also test: corrupt the 4-5 cross-reference by changing the 5-6 run's semanticContractVersion → should fail check (A). But wait, that would modify the 4-5 slice or the 5-6 slice? I should test by changing the 5-6 run's semanticContractVersion to a mismatched value. Actually, let me not over-test. The key corruption test is: does a corrupted slice FAIL?\n\nLet me do the corruption test on a COPY to avoid risking the real file. I'll copy the slice to a temp file, corrupt the temp file, and run the validator against the temp file using the `-SlicePath` parameter. Wait — but the validator also loads sibling slices from $PSScriptRoot, and the corrupted copy would be a different filename. The validator's `$SlicePath` param defaults to `$PSScriptRoot\\phase2-projections-slice.json`. I can pass `-SlicePath` to point at a corrupted copy. But the sibling harvest uses `Get-ChildItem -LiteralPath $PSScriptRoot -Filter \"*.json\"` and excludes the manifest and `$selfSliceBasename`. The `$selfSliceBasename` is derived from `$SlicePath`. So if I pass a corrupted copy as SlicePath, the self-slice basename would be the corrupted copy's name, and the ORIGINAL slice would be harvested as a sibling — causing id collisions (since the corrupted copy has the same ids as the original). That would produce false collisions.\n\nSo the cleanest corruption test: corrupt the REAL slice in place (after backing it up), run, then restore. Let me do that carefully.\n\nLet me test two corruptions:\n1. Remove \"mvp-contracts-stable\" from the denial receipt's com_decision_rationale → check (C) should fire.\n2. Flip ownsWorkflowState to true on plan 001 → existing check fires.\n\nActually, let me just do ONE comprehensive corruption that triggers multiple new checks to prove the new checks actually catch corruption. The most targeted: corrupt the denial receipt rationale (removes mvp-contracts-stable) → check (C) fires. Let me also verify that check (A) catches a version mismatch.\n\nLet me do this carefully with a backup and restore. I'll use cp to back up, edit, run, restore.\n\nActually, the safest approach: copy the slice to a backup, then use the edit tool to corrupt the real file, run the validator, then restore from backup using cp, then verify it passes again.\n\nLet me back up first.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_f70760d61001Q2vtSyHrQBoEH0","sessionID":"ses_08f91718dffe8QIoOmeSnVHPPW","messageID":"msg_f7075b726001EYq3ClfhNm5835"}}
