{"type":"text","timestamp":1784298342180,"sessionID":"ses_08f914172ffeudlWP9EWlAXTjJ","part":{"id":"prt_f7077ce77001lH9pmDTbkB5wuG","messageID":"msg_f70739fb8001J43DsbUmvUo6Qq","sessionID":"ses_08f914172ffeudlWP9EWlAXTjJ","type":"text","text":"I found a real defect. **T5 PASSED when it must FAIL**: when the proposal's `gapCitation` cites row `PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001-ROW-02` by id but describes a *different* row's gap (Power Automate content), the validator's \"substantive overlap\" check passes. \n\nRoot cause (validator lines 473-485): the fallback counts **isolated shared terms** (\"at least two significant terms\"). Generic vocabulary like `contract` and `semantics` recurs across many rows, so two isolated hits are satisfied even when the citation describes an unrelated candidate's gap. This is exactly the \"counted, not derived\" / \"self-asserted proof\" anti-pattern from the review kill list, and it fails the story requirement that the citation reference the *recorded gap in an evaluation row*.\n\nAll other corruption probes (T1-T4, T6-T7) correctly fail. Let me confirm T5 is the unique hole with a focused re-test, then fixit.","time":{"start":1784298327671,"end":1784298342174}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1784298344894,"sessionID":"ses_08f914172ffeudlWP9EWlAXTjJ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8b0e3a81dd3cc83b","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /home/doug/.ringer/work/bmad-hybrid/5-3-record-microsoft-platform-evaluation-evidence\ncp _bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/platform-evaluation-slice.json /tmp/pe-backup.json\nV=_bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/platform-evaluation-slice-validate.ps1\nexport DOTNET_ROOT=$HOME/.dotnet; export PATH=$HOME/.dotnet:$HOME/.dotnet/tools:$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH\necho \"=== T5-precise: point addressesGapInRow at Dataverse MCP row but cite Power Automate's gap words ===\"\npython3 -c \"\nimport json\nd=json.load(open('_bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/platform-evaluation-slice.json'))\np=d['platformEvaluationRun']['customServiceProposals'][0]\n# addressesGapInRow stays PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001-ROW-02 (Dataverse MCP, decision custom_gap_build)\n# but gapCitation text is borrowed from the Power Automate row (PE-LOCAL-EVAL-002-ROW-01) gap vocabulary\np['gapCitation']='Cites the recorded contract gap in evaluation row '+p['addressesGapInRow']+' about Power Automate consumption fit and idempotency semantics; the custom projection fills exactly this Microsoft-native gap.'\njson.dump(d,open('_bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/platform-evaluation-slice.json','w'),indent=2)\n\"\nout=$(pwsh -File \"$V\" 2>&1); rc=$?\necho \"RC=$rc (expected 1 = FAIL because citation does NOT match cited row's recorded gap)\"\necho \"$out\" | tail -3\ncp /tmp/pe-backup.json _bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/platform-evaluation-slice.json\necho \"=== restored baseline check ===\"\npwsh -File \"$V\" 2>&1 | tail -1"},"output":"=== T5-precise: point addressesGapInRow at Dataverse MCP row but cite Power Automate's gap words ===\nRC=0 (expected 1 = FAIL because citation does NOT match cited row's recorded gap)\nCustom proposals: 1\nReceipts: 3\nPLATFORM_EVALUATION_SLICE_VALIDATE_OK\n=== restored baseline check ===\nPLATFORM_EVALUATION_SLICE_VALIDATE_OK\n","metadata":{"output":"=== T5-precise: point addressesGapInRow at Dataverse MCP row but cite Power Automate's gap words ===\nRC=0 (expected 1 = FAIL because citation does NOT match cited row's recorded gap)\nCustom proposals: 1\nReceipts: 3\nPLATFORM_EVALUATION_SLICE_VALIDATE_OK\n=== restored baseline check ===\nPLATFORM_EVALUATION_SLICE_VALIDATE_OK\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd /home/doug/.ringer/work/bmad-hybrid/5-3-record-microsoft-platform-evaluation-evidence\ncp _bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/platform-evaluation-slice.json /tmp/pe-backup.json\nV=_bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/platform-evaluation-slice-validate.ps1\nexport DOTNET_ROOT=$HOME/.dotnet; export PATH=$HOME/.dotnet:$HOME/.dotnet/tools:$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH\necho \"=== T5-precise: point addressesGapInRow at Dataverse MCP row but cite Power Automate's gap words ===\"\npython3 -c \"\nimport json\nd=json.load(open('_bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/platform-evaluation-slice.json'))\np=d['platformEvaluationRun']['customServiceProposals'][0]\n# addressesGapInRow stays PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001-ROW-02 (Dataverse MCP, decision custom_gap_build)\n# but gapCitation text is borrowed from the Power Automate row (PE-LOCAL-EVAL-002-ROW-01) gap vocabulary\np['gapCitation']='Cites the recorded contract gap in evaluation row '+p['addressesGapInRow']+' about Power Automate consumption fit and idempotency semantics; the custom projection fills exactly this Microsoft-native gap.'\njson.dump(d,open('_bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/platform-evaluation-slice.json','w'),indent=2)\n\"\nout=$(pwsh -File \"$V\" 2>&1); rc=$?\necho \"RC=$rc (expected 1 = FAIL because citation does NOT match cited row's recorded gap)\"\necho \"$out\" | tail -3\ncp /tmp/pe-backup.json _bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/platform-evaluation-slice.json\necho \"=== restored baseline check ===\"\npwsh -File \"$V\" 2>&1 | tail -1","time":{"start":1784298344879,"end":1784298344888}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I found a real defect. **T5 PASSED (RC=0) when it should have FAILED.**\n\nT5: I changed the proposal's `gapCitation` to cite the row id (PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001-ROW-02) but used text from a DIFFERENT row (about Power Automate consumption fit, which is row PE-LOCAL-EVAL-002-ROW-01's gap text). The validator's \"substantive overlap\" check is supposed to ensure the citation references the CITED row's recorded gap. But it passed — meaning the check didn't catch the mismatch.\n\nWait, let me re-examine. Let me re-read my T5 corruption. I set:\n```\np['gapCitation']='Cites the recorded contract gap in evaluation row '+p['addressesGapInRow']+' about Power Automate consumption fit and idempotency semantics unrelated to the cited row.'\n```\nSo gapCitation = \"Cites the recorded contract gap in evaluation row PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001-ROW-02 about Power Automate consumption fit and idempotency semantics unrelated to the cited row.\"\n\nThe cited row is PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001-ROW-02 (Dataverse intelligence/MCP). Its `com_contract_gaps` is about Dataverse MCP exposing generic table-row CRUD semantics, append-only receipt ledger, meaning-graph projection.\n\nMy citation mentions \"Power Automate consumption fit and idempotency semantics\" — which does NOT overlap the cited row's gap text. So the validator SHOULD have flagged it as not substantively overlapping. But it PASSED (RC=0).\n\nWhy did it pass? Let me trace the logic:\n\n```powershell\n$gapNeedle = ($gapText -replace \"\\s+\", \" \").Trim()\n$verbatimHit = ($citation.IndexOf($gapNeedle, [System.StringComparison]::OrdinalIgnoreCase) -ge 0)\nif (-not $verbatimHit) {\n  $stopwords = @(...)\n  $tokens = [regex]::Matches($gapText, \"[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_/-]{3,}\") | ForEach-Object { $_.Value.ToLower() }\n  $significantTerms = @($tokens | Where-Object { $stopwords -notcontains $_ } | Sort-Object -Unique)\n  $matchedTerms = @($significantTerms | Where-Object { $citation -match [regex]::Escape($_) })\n  if ($matchedTerms.Count -lt 2) {\n    Add-Issue \"...must substantively reference...\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nSo it tokenizes the cited row's gap text, gets significant terms (length >= 4 after the first char, minus stopwords), and checks if at least 2 appear in the citation.\n\nThe cited row (PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001-ROW-02) gap text:\n\"Gap: Dataverse MCP exposes generic table-row CRUD semantics but projects no append-only receipt ledger view keyed by com_idempotency_key with com_before_state/com_after_state lineage, and no meaning-graph entity/edge projection matching the com_councilgraphentity/com_councilgraphedge contracts. The Council receipt contract and semantic contract require these projections; Dataverse MCP does not surface them natively, so a custom projection is required to fill this gap.\"\n\nSignificant terms (length >= 4, first char alpha, minus stopwords): dataverse, exposes (no, \"exposes\" is not in stopwords, length 7), generic, table-row (has hyphen, matches [A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_/-]{3,} → \"table-row\" wait the regex is on gapText... let me think. Actually `[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_/-]{3,}` matches words of >= 4 chars. \"table-row\" → \"table-row\" matches (t,a,b,l,e,-,r,o,w = 9 chars all in class). Hmm.\n\nMy citation: \"Cites the recorded contract gap in evaluation row PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001-ROW-02 about Power Automate consumption fit and idempotency semantics unrelated to the cited row.\"\n\nWhich significant terms from the cited row's gap appear in my citation? Let me find common words:\n- \"idempotency\" — the cited row's gap has \"com_idempotency_key\". The regex tokenizes \"com_idempotency_key\" → matches `[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_/-]{3,}` → \"com_idempotency_key\" (c,o,m,_,i,... all in class). Lowercased: \"com_idempotency_key\". My citation has \"idempotency\" (not \"com_idempotency_key\"). Does `com_idempotency_key` match as a substring? The check is `$citation -match [regex]::Escape($_)` where $_ = \"com_idempotency_key\". My citation has \"idempotency\" not \"com_idempotency_key\". So NO match.\n\nHmm wait. Let me reconsider what tokens come out of the gap text. The regex `[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_/-]{3,}` is greedy and matches the longest run. In \"com_idempotency_key\", the whole thing \"com_idempotency_key\" matches (all chars in [A-Za-z0-9_/-], length >= 4). So the token is \"com_idempotency_key\". My citation has \"idempotency\" — not a substring match of \"com_idempotency_key\" in my citation (my citation doesn't contain \"com_idempotency_key\"). \n\nWait, but does my citation contain \"com_idempotency_key\"? No. So that token doesn't match.\n\nBut the gap text also has standalone words. Let me think about which significant tokens from the gap text appear in my citation:\n- \"semantics\" — the gap text has \"CRUD semantics\". Token \"semantics\" (length 9). My citation has \"semantics\". MATCH! \n- Wait, is \"semantics\" a stopword? The stopword list doesn't include \"semantics\". So \"semantics\" is significant. My citation contains \"semantics\". So matchedTerms includes \"semantics\". That's 1.\n- \"append-only\" — token would be \"append-only\" (a,p,p,e,n,d,-,o,n,l,y = 11 chars). My citation doesn't have \"append-only\". No match.\n- \"receipt\" — gap text has \"receipt ledger\". Token \"receipt\" (length 7). Is \"receipt\" a stopword? No. My citation: does it contain \"receipt\"? My citation: \"Cites the recorded contract gap in evaluation row PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001-ROW-02 about Power Automate consumption fit and idempotency semantics unrelated to the cited row.\" — No \"receipt\". No match.\n- Wait, but actually — hold on. Let me re-read my citation. It says \"idempotency semantics\". The gap text has \"com_idempotency_key\" and \"semantics\". The token \"semantics\" matches (1). \n\nHmm, is there a second match? Let me look for another. My citation words: cites, recorded, contract, gap, evaluation, row, about, power, automate, consumption, fit, idempotency, semantics, unrelated, cited.\n\nGap text significant tokens that might match my citation:\n- \"semantics\" → matches \"semantics\" in citation. (1)\n- \"contract\" — gap text has \"contracts\" and \"contract\". Token from \"contracts\" = \"contracts\". Token from \"contract\" = \"contract\". My citation has \"contract\" (singular). Does `contracts` (escaped) match my citation \"contract\"? No — regex escape of \"contracts\" requires \"contracts\" exactly; my citation has \"contract\" not \"contracts\". Does `contract` (from \"...contract require\") match? The gap text has \"semantic contract require\" and \"receipt contract\". So token \"contract\" exists. My citation has \"contract\". MATCH! (2)\n\nWait, so \"contract\" and \"semantics\" both match. That's 2 significant terms → matchedTerms.Count = 2 → NOT < 2 → no issue. So the validator PASSES the substantive overlap check even though my citation is about a DIFFERENT row's content.\n\nHmm, but \"contract\" and \"semantics\" are generic terms that appear in BOTH the cited row's gap text AND my mismatched citation. That's the hole: **common/generic contract vocabulary terms (like \"contract\", \"semantics\", \"receipt\") satisfy the \"at least two significant terms\" threshold even when the citation is about an entirely different candidate's gap.**\n\nThis is a REAL defect in the substantive-overlap check. The threshold of \"2 significant terms\" is too weak — generic shared vocabulary (contract, semantics, receipt, projection, etc.) that appears across many rows can satisfy it, so a citation pointing at row A but describing row B's gap can pass.\n\nThis is exactly the kind of thing the review kill list targets: the gap-citation check is supposed to prove the citation references \"a recorded gap in an evaluation row,\" but the substantive-overlap heuristic is too permissive — it doesn't actually tie the citation to the SPECIFIC gap content of the cited row.\n\nSo I have a legitimate defect to fix. The fix: tighten the substantive-overlap check so it actually requires the citation to overlap the cited row's SPECIFIC gap content, not just 2 generic shared terms.\n\nHow to fix this robustly (general rule, not hardcoded)? Options:\n1. Require a HIGHER threshold of significant terms (e.g., more than 2). But that's still just counting.\n2. Require a verbatim multi-word run from the cited row's gap text (a \"phrase\" match), not just isolated terms. The current code checks `$verbatimHit` (full gap text as a substring) which is too strict (the whole gap text won't appear), then falls back to \"2 significant terms.\" A middle ground: require a verbatim run of N consecutive words (e.g., 3+ consecutive significant words) from the cited row's gap text to appear in the citation.\n\nOption 2 is stronger. But let me think about what the review would accept. The story says \"the custom proposal's gap citation references a recorded gap in an evaluation row.\" The validator must prove the citation actually references the recorded gap content.\n\nA robust approach: tokenize the cited row's gap text into consecutive significant-word phrases (e.g., 3-grams of significant terms), and require at least one such phrase to appear in the citation. This ties the citation to the specific sequencing of words in the cited gap, not just isolated shared vocabulary.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The simplest robust fix that closes the hole: require the citation to contain a verbatim run of at least 3 consecutive words from the cited row's gap text. Let me verify the current slice's gapCitation would still pass with this stricter check.\n\nThe slice's actual gapCitation (line 182):\n\"Cites the recorded contract gap in evaluation row PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001-ROW-02 (Dataverse intelligence/MCP, decision custom_gap_build): Dataverse MCP exposes generic table-row CRUD semantics but projects no append-only receipt ledger view keyed by com_idempotency_key with com_before_state/com_after_state lineage, and no meaning-graph entity/edge projection matching the com_councilgraphentity/com_councilgraphedge contracts. The custom Council Receipt Ledger projection fills exactly this Microsoft-native gap.\"\n\nThis contains a near-verbatim copy of the gap text: \"Dataverse MCP exposes generic table-row CRUD semantics but projects no append-only receipt ledger view keyed by com_idempotency_key with com_before_state/com_after_state lineage, and no meaning-graph entity/edge projection matching the com_councilgraphentity/com_councilgraphedge contracts.\" \n\nSo a verbatim-run check would PASS for the legitimate citation (it has a long verbatim run) and FAIL for my T5 mismatched citation (which has no verbatim run, only isolated shared terms).\n\nLet me design the fix:\n\nCurrent logic (lines 473-485):\n```powershell\nelse {\n  $gapNeedle = ($gapText -replace \"\\s+\", \" \").Trim()\n  $verbatimHit = ($citation.IndexOf($gapNeedle, [System.StringComparison]::OrdinalIgnoreCase) -ge 0)\n  if (-not $verbatimHit) {\n    $stopwords = @(...)\n    $tokens = [regex]::Matches($gapText, \"[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_/-]{3,}\") | ForEach-Object { $_.Value.ToLower() }\n    $significantTerms = @($tokens | Where-Object { $stopwords -notcontains $_ } | Sort-Object -Unique)\n    $matchedTerms = @($significantTerms | Where-Object { $citation -match [regex]::Escape($_) })\n    if ($matchedTerms.Count -lt 2) {\n      Add-Issue $issues \"$pSubject gapCitation must substantively reference the recorded gap text in row $gapRowId (a verbatim run or at least two significant terms drawn from that row's com_contract_gaps); found neither.\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe `$verbatimHit` checks if the ENTIRE gap text appears in the citation (too strict). The fallback is \"2 significant terms\" (too permissive).\n\nBetter fix: check for a verbatim run of consecutive words from the gap text. Approach: split the gap text into word tokens (preserving order), build consecutive n-grams (e.g., 3-word runs), normalize whitespace/case, and check if ANY 3-word run from the gap text appears in the citation. This requires actual sequencing overlap, closing the \"isolated generic terms\" hole.\n\nLet me design this carefully:\n\n```powershell\nelse {\n  # The citation must reference the SPECIFIC recorded gap, not just isolated generic\n  # vocabulary (contract / semantics / receipt) that recurs across many rows. A\n  # verbatim multi-word run drawn from the cited row's own com_contract_gaps ties the\n  # citation to that row's specific gap content. Isolated shared terms do not.\n  $stopwords = @(...)\n  # Build consecutive significant-word runs from the cited row's gap text.\n  $gapWords = [regex]::Matches($gapText, \"[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_/-]+\") | ForEach-Object { $_.Value.ToLower() }\n  $sigWordRun = [System.Collections.Generic.List[string]]::new()\n  foreach ($w in $gapWords) {\n    if ($stopwords -notcontains $w -and $w.Length -ge 4) { $sigWordRun.Add($w) | Out-Null }\n    else { $sigWordRun.Add($null) | Out-Null }  # break runs at non-significant words\n  }\n  # Build all 3-consecutive-significant-word phrases that actually appear contiguously\n  $normalizedCitation = [string]($citation -replace \"\\s+\", \" \")\n  $phraseHit = $false\n  ... check 3-grams ...\n}\n```\n\nHmm, this is getting complex. Let me think about a cleaner implementation.\n\nActually, a cleaner approach: check for a verbatim run of at least N (say 4) consecutive WORDS (any words, not just significant) from the gap text appearing in the citation. This is robust because a 4-word contiguous run is very unlikely to appear by accident in a mismatched citation.\n\nLet me reconsider. The current `$verbatimHit` requires the WHOLE gap text. I'll replace the fallback with a \"longest common substring of words\" check: split both gap text and citation into word sequences (lowercased, whitespace-normalized), and find the longest contiguous run of words from the gap text that appears in the citation. Require that run to be >= some threshold (e.g., 4 words).\n\nActually, the cleanest and most robust: extract all maximal runs of words from the gap text and check if any run of >= 4 consecutive words appears verbatim in the citation. This is a \"phrase containment\" check.\n\nLet me implement it as: normalize the gap text to a single-spaced lowercase string, split into words. Build 4-word sliding windows. For each window, join with single space and check if that phrase appears (case-insensitive) in the normalized citation. If any 4-word window matches → phraseHit = true.\n\nBut wait — I need to be careful about punctuation. The gap text has punctuation (commas, colons, semicolons, slashes). If I split on whitespace, \"CRUD\" and \"semantics\" stay, but \"com_idempotency_key\" stays as one token, and \"contracts.\" has a trailing period. The citation may or may not have the trailing period. So I should strip punctuation or normalize.\n\nLet me normalize: for both the gap text and citation, lowercase and replace non-alphanumeric (except internal underscores/hyphens) sequences with spaces, collapse whitespace. Then split into word tokens. Then build sliding windows.\n\nHmm, but \"com_before_state/com_after_state\" — the slash. If I replace slashes with spaces, the window changes. Let me keep it simple: I'll tokenize on whitespace after lowercasing and stripping trailing punctuation from each token.\n\nActually, the simplest robust approach that definitely works and passes the legitimate case:\n\nBuild 4-word windows from the gap text (after normalizing each token: lowercase, trim surrounding punctuation). Join window words with single space. Check if that joined phrase appears as a substring (case-insensitive) in the similarly-normalized citation.\n\nLet me verify with the legitimate citation: the gap text has the phrase \"Dataverse MCP exposes generic table-row CRUD semantics but projects no append-only receipt ledger view keyed by com_idempotency_key...\" and the citation contains almost exactly this. So a 4-word window like \"dataverse mcp exposes generic\" or \"mcp exposes generic table-row\" would appear in the citation. → phraseHit = true. PASS. \n\nFor my T5 mismatched citation: \"Cites the recorded contract gap in evaluation row PE-LOCAL-EVAL-001-ROW-02 about Power Automate consumption fit and idempotency semantics unrelated to the cited row.\" Does any 4-word run from the cited row's gap appear? The gap text 4-word runs include things like \"dataverse mcp exposes generic\", \"append only receipt ledger\", etc. My citation has none of these contiguous runs. The only shared words are isolated (\"contract\", \"semantics\"). → phraseHit = false. FAIL. \n\nSo the fix works for both cases. Let me set the threshold. What's a good minimum run length? 4 consecutive words is reasonable — accidental 4-word overlap is rare, but a genuine citation that quotes the gap will have it. But what if a citation doesn't quote verbatim but paraphrases substantively? The story requires the citation to \"cite a recorded gap.\" A paraphrase without any verbatim run is weaker evidence. The review kill list says \"no self-asserted booleans standing in for proof\" — a paraphrase-only citation with no verbatim tie to the gap is arguably \"standing in for proof.\" So requiring a verbatim run is defensible.\n\nBut I should keep a fallback: if no 4-word verbatim run, allow a HIGHER count of significant terms (e.g., >= 4 distinct significant terms) as an alternative. Actually, that reintroduces the hole somewhat (4 generic terms). Hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider. The cleanest, most defensible fix: require EITHER (a) a verbatim run of >= 4 consecutive words from the cited row's gap, OR (b) a verbatim run of >= 3 consecutive significant (non-stopword, length>=4) words. Option (b) is stricter on content (3 significant words in a row is content-specific) while (a) catches runs that include stopwords.\n\nActually, let me simplify to ONE robust rule: require a verbatim contiguous run of at least 4 word-tokens from the cited row's gap text to appear in the citation. This is clean, general, and closes the hole. Let me verify the legitimate citation passes and design edge cases.\n\nWait, but what about a citation that legitimately cites the gap but paraphrases? The existing slice's citation is nearly verbatim (it copies a long run). So requiring a verbatim run doesn't break the existing slice. And it's the right hardening: a citation should actually quote the recorded gap.\n\nHmm, but let me reconsider whether this is too strict and might be seen as \"hardcoded threshold magic.\" The threshold of 4 is a judgment call. Let me make it 3 to be safe — 3 consecutive words is still a strong signal and the legitimate citation easily exceeds it. Actually, the review cares about closing the hole, not the exact threshold. Let me think about what value closes T5 while passing the legit case.\n\nT5 mismatched citation shared words with the gap: \"contract\", \"semantics\" (isolated, non-consecutive in the gap). Are there any 2-consecutive-word runs shared? Let me check. The gap text has \"CRUD semantics\" (consecutive). My citation has \"idempotency semantics\" (not \"CRUD semantics\"). The gap has \"receipt contract\" — wait, let me check. Gap: \"...semantic contract require...\" → \"semantic contract\" consecutive. Citation: \"...contract gap...\" → \"contract gap\" not \"semantic contract\". Gap: \"append-only receipt ledger\" → \"receipt ledger\" consecutive. Citation has no \"receipt\". So no 2-word consecutive run shared. So even a threshold of 2 consecutive words would close T5.\n\nBut 2 consecutive words is weak (could be accidental). Let me use 3 consecutive word-tokens as the threshold — robust enough to avoid accidental matches, lenient enough for genuine citations. Actually, let me check: does the legit citation have a 3-consecutive-word run from the gap? Yes — \"dataverse mcp exposes\" (3 words), \"mcp exposes generic\", \"exposes generic table-row\", etc. Tons. So 3 works.\n\nLet me go with requiring a verbatim run of >= 3 consecutive word-tokens from the cited row's gap text appearing in the citation. I'll keep the \"isolated significant terms\" as a documented insufficient signal and remove it (or keep it only as a secondary fallback with a much higher bar).\n\nHmm, actually — let me reconsider the design to be maximally robust and review-proof. The review kill list says \"coverage derived not counted\" and \"no self-asserted booleans standing in for proof.\" The current \"2 significant terms\" is literally a COUNT of isolated terms — which is exactly the \"counted not derived\" anti-pattern AND it fails to actually prove citation-to-gap binding. \n\nThe fix: replace the isolated-term count with a verbatim contiguous-phrase containment check. This DERIVES that the citation actually quotes the recorded gap (a structural/content tie), rather than COUNTING coincidental shared vocabulary. This directly addresses the kill list.\n\nLet me write the replacement logic:\n\n```powershell\nelse {\n  # The citation must reference the SPECIFIC recorded gap content, not isolated\n  # generic vocabulary (contract / semantics / receipt) that recurs across many\n  # rows. Derive a structural tie: a verbatim contiguous run of word-tokens drawn\n  # from the cited row's own com_contract_gaps must appear in the citation. Counting\n  # isolated shared terms is insufficient — two unrelated rows can share \"contract\"\n  # and \"semantics\" without the citation describing the cited row's gap at all.\n  function Normalize-Tokens([string]$Text) {\n    $lower = $Text.ToLower()\n    $clean = $lower -replace '[^a-z0-9_/-]+', ' '\n    ($clean -replace '\\s+', ' ').Trim() -split ' ' | Where-Object { -not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($_) }\n  }\n  $gapTokens = Normalize-Tokens $gapText\n  $citationTokens = Normalize-Tokens $citation\n  $phraseHit = $false\n  $runLen = 3\n  for ($i = 0; $i -le $gapTokens.Count - $runLen; $i++) {\n    $phrase = ($gapTokens[$i..($i+$runLen-1)] -join ' ')\n    if ($citationTokens.Count -ge $runLen) {\n      for ($j = 0; $j -le $citationTokens.Count - $runLen; $j++) {\n        if (($citationTokens[$j..($j+$runLen-1)] -join ' ') -eq $phrase) { $phraseHit = $true; break }\n      }\n    }\n    if ($phraseHit) { break }\n  }\n  if (-not $phraseHit) {\n    Add-Issue $issues \"$pSubject gapCitation must substantively reference the recorded gap text in row $gapRowId (a verbatim run of at least $runLen consecutive word-tokens drawn from that row's com_contract_gaps); isolated shared vocabulary is not sufficient.\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWait — defining a function inside an else block is awkward in PowerShell. Let me inline the tokenization. Also, the existing code uses `$stopwords` — I can remove that since the phrase check doesn't need stopwords (consecutive runs are content-specific by nature).\n\nLet me also keep the existing `$verbatimHit` (full gap text substring) as a fast path — if the whole gap text appears, that's trivially a hit. Then add the phrase-run check.\n\nActually, the full-gap-text substring check (`$verbatimHit`) is a subset of the phrase check (if the whole text appears, 3-word runs appear too). So I can replace the whole block with just the phrase-run check. But keeping $verbatimHit as a fast path is harmless. Let me simplify: replace the fallback (isolated term count) with the phrase-run check, and keep $verbatimHit.\n\nHmm, but actually if I keep $verbatimHit (full text), and it's almost never true (citation rarely contains the ENTIRE gap text verbatim), then it falls through to the phrase check. The legit citation does NOT contain the entire gap text verbatim (it has \"...and no meaning-graph entity/edge projection matching the com_councilgraphentity/com_councilgraphedge contracts. The custom Council...\" — wait, the gap text ends with \"...so a custom projection is required to fill this gap.\" and the citation has \"...contracts. The custom Council Receipt Ledger projection fills exa