ever appears inside a\n+# normalized token (tokens are [a-z0-9_/-] only), so a delimited phrase can\n+# only match whole tokens, never a prefix of one (e.g. \"semantic\" must not\n+# match inside \"semantics\"). That is what makes the tie a true phrase match\n+# rather than a bare substring scan.\n+$phraseDelim = '|'\n+$citationBlob = $phraseDelim + ($citationTokens -join $phraseDelim) + $phraseDelim\n $phraseHit = $false\n $requiredRunLength = 3\n $lastStart = $gapTokens.Count - $requiredRunLength\n for ($i = 0; $i -le $lastStart; $i++) {\n-  $phrase = ($gapTokens[$i..($i + $requiredRunLength - 1)] -join ' ')\n-  if ($normCitation.IndexOf($phrase, [System.StringComparison]::Ordinal) -ge 0) {\n+  $phrase = $phraseDelim + ($gapTokens[$i..($i + $requiredRunLength - 1)] -join $phraseDelim) + $phraseDelim\n+  if ($citationBlob.IndexOf($phrase, [System.StringComparison]::Ordinal) -ge 0) {\n     $phraseHit = $true\n     break\n   }\n }\n","filediff":{"file":"/home/doug/.ringer/work/bmad-hybrid/5-3-record-microsoft-platform-evaluation-evidence/_bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/platform-evaluation-slice-validate.ps1","patch":"Index: /home/doug/.ringer/work/bmad-hybrid/5-3-record-microsoft-platform-evaluation-evidence/_bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/platform-evaluation-slice-validate.ps1\n===================================================================\n--- /home/doug/.ringer/work/bmad-hybrid/5-3-record-microsoft-platform-evaluation-evidence/_bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/platform-evaluation-slice-validate.ps1\n+++ /home/doug/.ringer/work/bmad-hybrid/5-3-record-microsoft-platform-evaluation-evidence/_bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/platform-evaluation-slice-validate.ps1\n@@ -482,17 +482,24 @@\n # across many rows and would let a citation describing a different row's gap\n # pass. Both strings are normalized identically so the comparison is purely\n # content-driven.\n $normCitation = [string]((([string]$citation).ToLower() -replace '[^a-z0-9_/-]+', ' ') -replace '\\s+', ' ')\n-$normCitation = $normCitation.Trim()\n+$citationTokens = @($normCitation.Trim() -split ' ' | Where-Object { -not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($_) })\n $normGap = [string]((([string]$gapText).ToLower() -replace '[^a-z0-9_/-]+', ' ') -replace '\\s+', ' ')\n $gapTokens = @($normGap.Trim() -split ' ' | Where-Object { -not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($_) })\n+# Token-boundary-aware containment: the delimiter never appears inside a\n+# normalized token (tokens are [a-z0-9_/-] only), so a delimited phrase can\n+# only match whole tokens, never a prefix of one (e.g. \"semantic\" must not\n+# match inside \"semantics\"). That is what makes the tie a true phrase match\n+# rather than a bare substring scan.\n+$phraseDelim = '|'\n+$citationBlob = $phraseDelim + ($citationTokens -join $phraseDelim) + $phraseDelim\n $phraseHit = $false\n $requiredRunLength = 3\n $lastStart = $gapTokens.Count - $requiredRunLength\n for ($i = 0; $i -le $lastStart; $i++) {\n-  $phrase = ($gapTokens[$i..($i + $requiredRunLength - 1)] -join ' ')\n-  if ($normCitation.IndexOf($phrase, [System.StringComparison]::Ordinal) -ge 0) {\n+  $phrase = $phraseDelim + ($gapTokens[$i..($i + $requiredRunLength - 1)] -join $phraseDelim) + $phraseDelim\n+  if ($citationBlob.IndexOf($phrase, [System.StringComparison]::Ordinal) -ge 0) {\n     $phraseHit = $true\n     break\n   }\n }\n","additions":10,"deletions":3},"truncated":false},"title":"_bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/platform-evaluation-slice-validate.ps1","time":{"start":1784298608292,"end":1784298608297}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Found it. The matching phrase is \"contract and semantic\". \n\nIn the gap: \"...the council receipt **contract and semantic** contract require these projections...\" → tokens \"contract\", \"and\", \"semantic\" are contiguous.\n\nIn the T5b citation: \"...because the **contract and semantics** require a projection...\" → normalized tokens: \"contract\", \"and\", \"semantics\". \n\nWait — but the phrase is \"contract and semantic\" (singular \"semantic\") and the citation has \"contract and semantics\" (plural \"semantics\"). How does \"contract and semantic\" appear as a substring in \"contract and semantics require a projection\"?\n\nBecause \"contract and semantic\" is a substring of \"contract and semantics\"! The token \"semantics\" contains \"semantic\" as a prefix. Since I'm doing a substring match on the normalized string (not a token-boundary-aware match), \"contract and semantic\" matches inside \"contract and semantics require\" (the \"semantic\" is a prefix of \"semantics\").\n\nThis is the classic substring-vs-token-boundary bug. My `.IndexOf($phrase)` matches across token boundaries because \"semantic\" is a prefix of \"semantics\". \n\nThis is actually a SECOND bug in my fix: the substring approach allows partial-token matches. I need a token-boundary-aware comparison.\n\nFix: instead of `IndexOf` substring on the normalized string, I should check if the phrase appears as a sequence of COMPLETE tokens in the citation's token list. I.e., build the citation tokens list and check if any 3-token window in the citation EXACTLY equals the gap phrase (token by token).\n\nThis is what my earlier design considered (comparing citationTokens[j..] to gapTokens[i..]) but I simplified to substring. The simplification introduced the partial-token bug. Let me switch to token-window comparison.\n\nLet me rewrite the check:\n```powershell\n$normCitation = ...\n$normGap = ...\n$gapTokens = @($normGap.Trim() -split ' ' | Where-Object { -not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($_) })\n$citationTokens = @($normCitation.Trim() -split ' ' | Where-Object { -not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($_) })\n$phraseHit = $false\n$requiredRunLength = 3\n$lastGapStart = $gapTokens.Count - $requiredRunLength\n$lastCitStart = $citationTokens.Count - $requiredRunLength\nfor ($i = 0; $i -le $lastGapStart; $i++) {\n  for ($j = 0; $j -le $lastCitStart; $j++) {\n    $match = $true\n    for ($k = 0; $k -lt $requiredRunLength; $k++) {\n      if ($gapTokens[$i + $k] -ne $citationTokens[$j + $k]) { $match = $false; break }\n    }\n    if ($match) { $phraseHit = $true; break }\n  }\n  if ($phraseHit) { break }\n}\n```\n\nThis is O(n*m*k) but the texts are small (gap ~50 tokens, citation ~30 tokens). Fine.\n\nAlternatively, simpler: build the citation as a normalized string but with explicit token delimiters that can't be prefixes — e.g., wrap each token in delimiters. Like `$normCitation = '|' + ($citationTokens -join '|') + '|'` and phrase = `'|' + ($gapTokens[i..] -join '|') + '|'`. Then substring match is token-boundary-safe because '|' delimiters ensure whole-token matching. Hmm, but if a token is a prefix of another, \"semantic|\" vs \"semantics|\" — the delimiter prevents \"semantic\" from matching inside \"semantics\". Let me use that approach — it's simpler than nested loops:\n\n```powershell\n$delim = [char]31  # unit separator, won't appear in text\n$citBlob = $delim + ($citationTokens -join $delim) + $delim\n...\n$phrase = $delim + ($gapTokens[$i..($i+$runLen-1)] -join $delim) + $delim\nif ($citBlob.Contains($phrase)) { ... }\n```\n\nActually, using a delimiter character approach with a rare char is clean and token-boundary-safe. But [char]31 might be finicky. Let me use a simpler delimiter like a pipe. Since tokens are normalized to [a-z0-9_/-], the pipe `|` never appears in any token. So:\n\n```powershell\n$citationBlob = '|' + ($citationTokens -join '|') + '|'\nfor (...) {\n  $phrase = '|' + ($gapTokens[$i..($i+$runLen-1)] -join '|') + '|'\n  if ($citationBlob.Contains($phrase)) { $phraseHit = $true; break }\n}\n```\n\nWait, but a phrase in the middle of the citation: citationBlob = \"|references|row|pe-local-eval-001-row-02|because|the|contract|and|semantics|require|a|projection|this|is|the|recorded|gap|\". Phrase for gap tokens \"contract\",\"and\",\"semantic\" = \"|contract|and|semantic|\". Does \"|contract|and|semantic|\" appear in the citation blob? The citation blob has \"|contract|and|semantics|\" (with the 's'). \"|contract|and|semantic|\" vs \"|contract|and|semantics|\" — the first ends with \"semantic|\" but citation has \"semantics|\". So \"|contract|and|semantic|\" is NOT a substring (because after \"semantic\" the blob has \"s|\" not \"|\"). \n\nThis correctly REJECTS the partial-token match. And for the legitimate citation, the gap phrase \"dataverse mcp exposes\" = \"|dataverse|mcp|exposes|\" appears in the legit citation blob \"|...|dataverse|mcp|exposes|generic|...|\". MATCH. \n\nSo the delimiter approach is clean, token-boundary-safe, and O(n) per phrase. Let me use it.\n\nBut wait — there's still the question: does the legitimate citation contain a 3-run from the gap? Yes (\"dataverse mcp exposes\" etc.). And does the delimiter approach find it? Yes. Let me verify with the legit citation after the fix.\n\nLet me also reconsider T5 (cite wrong row's content): citation \"...about power automate consumption fit and idempotency semantics...\" — does any gap 3-run appear (token-boundary-aware)? No. → FAIL. Good.\n\nAnd T5b: \"|contract|and|semantic|\" not in citation blob (has \"semantics\"). → FAIL. Good.\n\nLet me rewrite the else block with the delimiter approach. Let me re-edit the block I just wrote.\n\nLet me reconstruct the current state of the block (after my edit) and replace the tokenization/match part.\n\nCurrent block (after my edit):\n```powershell\n      else {\n        # Derive a structural tie between the citation and the cited row's recorded\n        # gap text: a verbatim contiguous run of word-tokens drawn from that row's own\n        # com_contract_gaps must appear in the citation. A contiguous run (not a count\n        # of isolated terms) is required because isolated generic vocabulary recurs\n        # across many rows and would let a citation describing a different row's gap\n        # pass. Both strings are normalized